WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.750 --> 00:00:04.193
Addiction doesn't discriminate.

00:00:05.140 --> 00:00:07.310
It doesn't care how rich, how successful,

00:00:07.310 --> 00:00:09.320
how fit or good looking you are.

00:00:09.320 --> 00:00:11.620
It doesn't care if you deny having it.

00:00:11.620 --> 00:00:16.620
But if you are truly indeed a drug addict or an alcoholic,

00:00:17.930 --> 00:00:20.896
it will take you down every time.

00:00:20.896 --> 00:00:23.479
(upbeat music)

00:00:26.280 --> 00:00:28.193
20 years ago, I was that guy.

00:00:28.193 --> 00:00:31.670
I was broken, lost, utterly alone.

00:00:31.670 --> 00:00:33.170
My mornings typically began

00:00:33.170 --> 00:00:35.090
with a vodka tonic in the shower,

00:00:35.090 --> 00:00:37.980
it generally ended up in a blackout coming too

00:00:37.980 --> 00:00:40.670
and all manner of strange compromising situations

00:00:40.670 --> 00:00:43.500
with no idea what I'd done or where I'd been.

00:00:43.500 --> 00:00:47.122
Other than this vague, but very real feeling

00:00:47.122 --> 00:00:50.340
of just incomprehensible demoralization.

00:00:50.340 --> 00:00:53.560
There was nothing sexy, romantic or rock and roll about it.

00:00:53.560 --> 00:00:55.903
It was really just sad and pathetic.

00:00:56.830 --> 00:00:58.450
And of course I knew I was an alcoholic.

00:00:58.450 --> 00:00:59.563
I'd known it forever.

00:01:00.650 --> 00:01:03.020
But no matter how many times I tried to stop,

00:01:03.020 --> 00:01:06.310
I just couldn't because there's a huge difference

00:01:06.310 --> 00:01:10.470
between self knowledge and actual change.

00:01:10.470 --> 00:01:12.694
I was unable to change

00:01:12.694 --> 00:01:16.030
until I was willing to let go of everything,

00:01:16.030 --> 00:01:18.040
everything I thought I knew about how to live,

00:01:18.040 --> 00:01:20.270
how to think, how to be,

00:01:20.270 --> 00:01:24.793
and simply raise my hand and let other people help me.

00:01:26.050 --> 00:01:28.800
Amazingly, I did find a way out.

00:01:28.800 --> 00:01:31.360
And today my life is just a miracle.

00:01:31.360 --> 00:01:33.540
A miracle I could have not possibly imagined

00:01:33.540 --> 00:01:36.840
when I came to in that treatment center 23 years ago.

00:01:36.840 --> 00:01:39.410
So if you're one of the many millions out there

00:01:39.410 --> 00:01:43.230
that's suffering in the darkness, unable to grasp the light,

00:01:43.230 --> 00:01:44.810
here's the truth.

00:01:44.810 --> 00:01:48.340
The truth is, you never have to use or drink again.

00:01:48.340 --> 00:01:50.150
There is a solution.

00:01:50.150 --> 00:01:52.420
It's a solution that's available to all,

00:01:52.420 --> 00:01:55.450
no matter how dire your circumstances.

00:01:55.450 --> 00:01:59.030
And that solution begins with a decision.

00:01:59.030 --> 00:02:01.320
A decision to set aside that fear of the unknown,

00:02:01.320 --> 00:02:03.923
raise your hand and ask for help.

00:02:05.010 --> 00:02:08.543
It's saved my life, and it can save yours too.

00:02:28.610 --> 00:02:32.160
dealing with addiction in some form living today.

00:02:32.160 --> 00:02:35.630
Given this ubiquity, I felt compelled to dedicate

00:02:35.630 --> 00:02:39.290
this third installment of our deep dive masterclass series

00:02:39.290 --> 00:02:41.860
to share stories and share wisdom

00:02:41.860 --> 00:02:43.720
on the subject of addiction,

00:02:43.720 --> 00:02:46.890
from some of the best guests I've ever had on the show,

00:02:46.890 --> 00:02:48.430
and to shedding light on a topic

00:02:48.430 --> 00:02:51.310
that has in so many countless ways

00:02:51.310 --> 00:02:54.030
shaped the trajectory of my life.

00:02:54.030 --> 00:02:57.360
For those unaware of this new semi-regular format,

00:02:57.360 --> 00:03:01.170
what follows is a compilation of 10 incredible

00:03:01.170 --> 00:03:05.100
and unique perspectives on addiction, on recovery,

00:03:05.100 --> 00:03:07.340
taken from previous episodes.

00:03:07.340 --> 00:03:09.620
And it is my hope that these stories

00:03:09.620 --> 00:03:13.470
can bring you greater understanding, greater empathy,

00:03:13.470 --> 00:03:16.830
and perhaps even a modicum of peace.

00:03:16.830 --> 00:03:18.500
And for those currently suffering,

00:03:18.500 --> 00:03:22.640
even motivation to stop using and get help.

00:03:22.640 --> 00:03:25.630
Our recent episode with Dr. Anna Lembke

00:03:25.630 --> 00:03:27.710
seems like the best way to begin.

00:03:27.710 --> 00:03:29.330
Anna is a professor of psychiatry

00:03:29.330 --> 00:03:31.290
at Stanford University School of Medicine,

00:03:31.290 --> 00:03:33.670
and chief of the Stanford Addiction Medicine

00:03:33.670 --> 00:03:35.500
Dual Diagnosis Clinic.

00:03:35.500 --> 00:03:39.120
She is widely published, has testified before Congress,

00:03:39.120 --> 00:03:41.277
and has authored two important books;

00:03:41.277 --> 00:03:42.940
"Drug Dealer, MD,"

00:03:42.940 --> 00:03:45.080
and her newest, "Dopamine Nation."

00:03:45.080 --> 00:03:47.340
Which is this really powerful primer

00:03:47.340 --> 00:03:50.570
on how to moderate compulsive over-consumption

00:03:50.570 --> 00:03:53.850
in a world where unfortunately feeling good

00:03:53.850 --> 00:03:57.040
has become confused with the highest good.

00:03:57.040 --> 00:04:00.470
Our original conversation was important, it was impactful.

00:04:00.470 --> 00:04:03.780
And truly an addiction primer within itself

00:04:03.780 --> 00:04:06.300
worthy of adding to your queue for later.

00:04:06.300 --> 00:04:09.040
But for now, please enjoy this powerful clip

00:04:09.040 --> 00:04:11.443
from my interview with Dr. Anna Lembke.

00:04:12.930 --> 00:04:16.420
So why don't we define our terms here a little bit?

00:04:16.420 --> 00:04:18.513
Like how do you define addiction?

00:04:19.570 --> 00:04:24.570
I define addiction as the continued compulsive use

00:04:25.010 --> 00:04:28.820
of a drug or behavior despite harm to self

00:04:28.820 --> 00:04:30.560
and/or others.

00:04:30.560 --> 00:04:31.393
How do you define it?

00:04:31.393 --> 00:04:33.620
I mean, that's the standard definition.

00:04:33.620 --> 00:04:35.109
Yeah?

00:04:35.109 --> 00:04:35.942
Yeah.

00:04:35.942 --> 00:04:40.470
And how does that, like, as I mentioned at the opening,

00:04:40.470 --> 00:04:43.330
there's alcoholism, there's drug addiction.

00:04:43.330 --> 00:04:45.120
There are some qualitative differences

00:04:45.120 --> 00:04:49.810
between substance addiction and behavioral addictions.

00:04:49.810 --> 00:04:51.270
And now that we're in this world

00:04:51.270 --> 00:04:55.370
where everything has an addictive allure to it,

00:04:55.370 --> 00:04:59.630
do you qualify our relationship with our devices

00:04:59.630 --> 00:05:02.560
and online shopping and gambling in the same way?

00:05:02.560 --> 00:05:06.730
Like, with this idea of addiction being a spectrum,

00:05:06.730 --> 00:05:09.790
how does it differ in terms of like our relationship

00:05:09.790 --> 00:05:14.230
to Twitter versus our relationship to heroin?

00:05:14.230 --> 00:05:16.760
I don't really see all that much difference

00:05:16.760 --> 00:05:17.950
between those things.

00:05:17.950 --> 00:05:20.490
I think it's just a matter of degree.

00:05:20.490 --> 00:05:22.283
I mean, I've had, for example,

00:05:22.283 --> 00:05:27.283
journalists writing for esteemed publications call me

00:05:28.050 --> 00:05:29.980
to interview me about sex addiction.

00:05:29.980 --> 00:05:32.460
And say to me, "Well, it's not really an addiction, is it?

00:05:32.460 --> 00:05:36.290
I mean, it's really just about cultural morals around

00:05:36.290 --> 00:05:39.280
what's acceptable sexual behavior and what isn't."

00:05:39.280 --> 00:05:42.317
And I've had to correct that person say,

00:05:42.317 --> 00:05:44.470
"No, you're wrong.

00:05:44.470 --> 00:05:46.670
This is really an addiction.

00:05:46.670 --> 00:05:48.760
It can devastate lives.

00:05:48.760 --> 00:05:52.100
People can lose their lives over these problems."

00:05:52.100 --> 00:05:54.460
I mean, this is what I really wanna drive home to people;

00:05:54.460 --> 00:05:57.070
like, yeah, there are many risk factors for addiction,

00:05:57.070 --> 00:06:00.080
but you can have none of those risk factors in today's world

00:06:00.080 --> 00:06:01.670
and get really addictive.

00:06:01.670 --> 00:06:05.265
Well, let's talk about the neurochemistry of addiction.

00:06:05.265 --> 00:06:08.430
Walk me through what's happening in our brains

00:06:08.430 --> 00:06:10.730
and the role that dopamine plays in all of this.

00:06:10.730 --> 00:06:14.340
Yeah, so dopamine is a neurotransmitter,

00:06:14.340 --> 00:06:16.210
which means that it is the molecule

00:06:16.210 --> 00:06:18.560
that allows the electrical signal

00:06:18.560 --> 00:06:20.450
from the presynaptic neuron

00:06:20.450 --> 00:06:23.143
to be communicated to the postsynaptic neuron,

00:06:23.143 --> 00:06:24.440
because there's a little gap called the synapse

00:06:24.440 --> 00:06:26.111
between those two neurons.

00:06:26.111 --> 00:06:28.860
So neuro-transmitters allow fine tuning

00:06:28.860 --> 00:06:30.580
of those electrical signals.

00:06:30.580 --> 00:06:33.160
And dopamine is the most important neurotransmitter

00:06:33.160 --> 00:06:36.150
involved in motivation and reward.

00:06:36.150 --> 00:06:37.790
And the fundamental difference

00:06:37.790 --> 00:06:40.590
between things that are addictive and those that aren't,

00:06:40.590 --> 00:06:42.250
is that things that are addictive

00:06:42.250 --> 00:06:44.361
release a lot more dopamine.

00:06:44.361 --> 00:06:46.920
So we have dopamine firing in our brain,

00:06:46.920 --> 00:06:49.530
that occurs at a tonic baseline.

00:06:49.530 --> 00:06:52.500
And when we do something that's rewarding or pleasurable,

00:06:52.500 --> 00:06:56.380
we get a little rise in dopamine levels or a spike.

00:06:56.380 --> 00:06:59.540
So for example, chocolate increases dopamine levels

00:06:59.540 --> 00:07:02.200
about 50% above baseline.

00:07:02.200 --> 00:07:03.920
Sex is about 100%.

00:07:03.920 --> 00:07:06.200
Nicotine is about 150%.

00:07:06.200 --> 00:07:09.690
And things like methamphetamines are 1000%.

00:07:09.690 --> 00:07:12.620
Really, you have to imagine that in your brain,

00:07:12.620 --> 00:07:15.580
there's a balance, like a teeter-totter and a playground.

00:07:15.580 --> 00:07:18.310
When we experience pleasure, the balance tips one way,

00:07:18.310 --> 00:07:21.712
when we experience pain, it tips the other.

00:07:21.712 --> 00:07:25.360
But one of the fundamental rules governing that balance

00:07:25.360 --> 00:07:28.020
is that it wants to remain level.

00:07:28.020 --> 00:07:30.700
So with any deviation from neutrality,

00:07:30.700 --> 00:07:33.730
the brain will work very hard to restore a level of balance

00:07:33.730 --> 00:07:36.113
or what's called homeostasis.

00:07:37.070 --> 00:07:39.750
So for example, if I do something pleasurable,

00:07:39.750 --> 00:07:41.590
like eat a piece of chocolate,

00:07:41.590 --> 00:07:43.140
I get a little tip to the side of pleasure,

00:07:43.140 --> 00:07:44.430
a little release of dopamine.

00:07:44.430 --> 00:07:46.480
But no sooner than that has happened,

00:07:46.480 --> 00:07:49.040
then my brain adapts to that phenomenon

00:07:49.040 --> 00:07:52.250
by down-regulating my own dopamine receptors,

00:07:52.250 --> 00:07:54.620
down-regulating my own dopamine transmission.

00:07:54.620 --> 00:07:56.480
And I imagine that as these little gremlins

00:07:56.480 --> 00:07:58.770
hopping on the pain side of the balance

00:07:58.770 --> 00:08:00.590
to bring it level again.

00:08:00.590 --> 00:08:01.560
But the thing about the gremlins

00:08:01.560 --> 00:08:02.810
is they like it on the balance,

00:08:02.810 --> 00:08:05.070
ao they stay on until the balance is tipped

00:08:05.070 --> 00:08:08.260
an equal and opposite amount to the side of pain.

00:08:08.260 --> 00:08:10.850
And that's called the opponent process reaction,

00:08:10.850 --> 00:08:14.467
the hangover, the come down, the after effect.

00:08:14.467 --> 00:08:16.570
And in my case, that's that moment of wanting

00:08:16.570 --> 00:08:17.920
another piece of chocolate.

00:08:18.790 --> 00:08:21.080
If I wait long enough, the gremlin hotspot hops off

00:08:21.080 --> 00:08:22.188
and balance's restored.

00:08:22.188 --> 00:08:24.850
But if I continue to consume chocolate

00:08:24.850 --> 00:08:27.780
in ever larger amounts to overcome the tolerance

00:08:27.780 --> 00:08:29.960
of the number of gremlins on the pain side,

00:08:29.960 --> 00:08:31.280
then I end up with enough gremlins

00:08:31.280 --> 00:08:34.380
on the pain side of my balance to fill this whole room.

00:08:34.380 --> 00:08:37.540
And I'm essentially in a dopamine deficit state

00:08:37.540 --> 00:08:40.430
with a balanced tilted to the side of pain.

00:08:40.430 --> 00:08:43.240
Now I have to keep using not to feel good,

00:08:43.240 --> 00:08:45.540
but just to feel normal.

00:08:45.540 --> 00:08:48.630
And when I stop using, my balance tips hard

00:08:48.630 --> 00:08:51.300
to the side of pain, I'm irritable, I'm depressed,

00:08:51.300 --> 00:08:53.490
I'm anxious, I can't sleep.

00:08:53.490 --> 00:08:55.660
Those are the universal symptoms of withdrawal

00:08:55.660 --> 00:08:57.780
from any addictive substance.

00:08:57.780 --> 00:09:00.300
And that can last a long time.

00:09:00.300 --> 00:09:04.760
We live in a world in which we are saturated with dopamine

00:09:04.760 --> 00:09:08.300
and we live in a culture which encourages us to pursue it.

00:09:08.300 --> 00:09:11.690
But the ultimate end result of pursuing dopamine

00:09:11.690 --> 00:09:14.330
is to feel worse than when you started.

00:09:14.330 --> 00:09:17.162
And this is really the central message.

00:09:17.162 --> 00:09:22.162
People are more depressed, more anxious, more suicidal,

00:09:22.650 --> 00:09:25.330
and more addicted than they were 30 years ago.

00:09:25.330 --> 00:09:28.050
And I contend that one of the main reasons

00:09:28.050 --> 00:09:32.260
is because of this relentless pursuit of pleasure

00:09:32.260 --> 00:09:35.900
that essentially adjusts the dopamine levels,

00:09:35.900 --> 00:09:39.840
changes the hedonic or pleasure set point

00:09:39.840 --> 00:09:43.270
to make people anhedonic, meaning without joy.

00:09:43.270 --> 00:09:44.583
Yeah.

00:09:44.583 --> 00:09:48.183
And one curious thing for me back to the neurochemistry is,

00:09:49.350 --> 00:09:50.880
you know, why someone becomes,

00:09:50.880 --> 00:09:54.110
if this dopamine neurotransmitter pathway

00:09:54.110 --> 00:09:59.000
that you're speaking about seems to be a general quality

00:09:59.000 --> 00:10:01.420
of all humans, why do some people get addicted

00:10:01.420 --> 00:10:02.660
and why some people don't.

00:10:02.660 --> 00:10:06.142
And for example, like alcohol is my drug of choice,

00:10:06.142 --> 00:10:10.510
gambling which debilitates a lot of people

00:10:10.510 --> 00:10:12.820
carries no charge for me at all.

00:10:12.820 --> 00:10:15.110
Like I just couldn't be less interested in it.

00:10:15.110 --> 00:10:16.990
Like, how does that work?

00:10:16.990 --> 00:10:20.000
Is that where genetics and nurturing

00:10:20.000 --> 00:10:22.260
and all kinds of other things come into play?

00:10:22.260 --> 00:10:25.940
I think we're all gonna get addicted to something,

00:10:25.940 --> 00:10:29.370
because now that special key that works

00:10:29.370 --> 00:10:31.940
for each of our individual locks, it's out there somewhere,

00:10:31.940 --> 00:10:34.360
and the worldwide web will allow us to find it.

00:10:34.360 --> 00:10:37.950
Having said that, it is true that people bring

00:10:37.950 --> 00:10:40.340
different degrees of vulnerability

00:10:40.340 --> 00:10:42.230
to the process of addiction.

00:10:42.230 --> 00:10:45.250
We do know that about 50 to 60% of the risk

00:10:45.250 --> 00:10:47.370
of becoming addicted is genetic.

00:10:47.370 --> 00:10:49.020
That's based on family studies,

00:10:49.020 --> 00:10:51.680
showing that if you have a biological parent or grandparent

00:10:51.680 --> 00:10:54.540
addicted to alcohol, you are at increased risk

00:10:54.540 --> 00:10:56.020
of becoming an alcoholic yourself,

00:10:56.020 --> 00:10:58.810
even if you're raised outside of the alcoholic home

00:10:58.810 --> 00:11:00.540
in a non-using home.

00:11:00.540 --> 00:11:02.340
So that's powerful genetics.

00:11:02.340 --> 00:11:06.150
It's polygenic, it's complex, we don't fully understand it.

00:11:06.150 --> 00:11:08.852
It's thought to be related to things like impulse control,

00:11:08.852 --> 00:11:13.852
ability to delay gratification, emotional dysregulation.

00:11:13.980 --> 00:11:16.160
But we don't really know what it is.

00:11:16.160 --> 00:11:17.530
Other risk factors include

00:11:17.530 --> 00:11:19.290
co-occurring psychiatric disorders.

00:11:19.290 --> 00:11:20.830
People with psychiatric disorders

00:11:20.830 --> 00:11:23.040
are more likely to develop an addiction.

00:11:23.040 --> 00:11:24.620
And also how you were raised.

00:11:24.620 --> 00:11:27.010
If you had a traumatic experience, as we've talked about,

00:11:27.010 --> 00:11:29.050
that puts you at risk.

00:11:29.050 --> 00:11:31.560
If you have parents who have explicitly

00:11:31.560 --> 00:11:34.350
or implicitly condoned substance use,

00:11:34.350 --> 00:11:37.040
either for recreation or as a coping strategy,

00:11:37.040 --> 00:11:38.440
that puts you at risk.

00:11:38.440 --> 00:11:41.870
Things like poverty, unemployment, that puts you at risk.

00:11:41.870 --> 00:11:44.120
So there are lots and lots of risk factors.

00:11:44.120 --> 00:11:47.877
But I think that the major risk factor in the modern world

00:11:47.877 --> 00:11:51.720
and one which is generally ignored, is simple access.

00:11:51.720 --> 00:11:53.870
If you have access to a drug,

00:11:53.870 --> 00:11:55.840
you are more likely to try it

00:11:55.840 --> 00:11:57.740
and more likely to get addicted to it.

00:11:57.740 --> 00:11:58.940
And now, as we've talked about,

00:11:58.940 --> 00:12:02.130
we live in a world of virtually infinite access.

00:12:02.130 --> 00:12:04.040
Yeah, you say, there's a quote in the book

00:12:04.040 --> 00:12:06.260
something along the lines of,

00:12:06.260 --> 00:12:07.750
there is something that will addict you

00:12:07.750 --> 00:12:09.360
and it's coming to a website near you.

00:12:09.360 --> 00:12:10.769
Right, you're right.

00:12:10.769 --> 00:12:12.340
Yeah.

00:12:12.340 --> 00:12:14.290
I don't know about you, but I found that conversation

00:12:14.290 --> 00:12:16.240
to be an absolute gift.

00:12:16.240 --> 00:12:18.340
And with that definition

00:12:18.340 --> 00:12:20.850
and understanding of addiction in mind,

00:12:20.850 --> 00:12:23.350
we move now to a firsthand account

00:12:23.350 --> 00:12:26.710
of what life as an addict is actually like.

00:12:26.710 --> 00:12:29.690
And we began with an account of what is perhaps

00:12:29.690 --> 00:12:33.340
the most powerful neurochemical dependence,

00:12:33.340 --> 00:12:35.210
opioid addiction.

00:12:35.210 --> 00:12:36.650
Here's what we know.

00:12:36.650 --> 00:12:40.560
There were nearly 70,000 overdose deaths in 2020 alone

00:12:40.560 --> 00:12:42.580
that involved opioids.

00:12:42.580 --> 00:12:47.150
Every hour of every day, six people in the US die

00:12:47.150 --> 00:12:49.040
from an opioid overdose.

00:12:49.040 --> 00:12:52.430
Opioid addiction is a tragic epidemic of untold proportions.

00:12:52.430 --> 00:12:56.460
And it's also precipitated this massive public health crisis

00:12:56.460 --> 00:12:59.060
destroying millions of lives unnecessarily,

00:12:59.060 --> 00:13:01.260
while also taxing our economy

00:13:01.260 --> 00:13:04.823
at the rate of $78.5 billion per year.

00:13:05.710 --> 00:13:08.410
This next clip chronicles one man's journey

00:13:08.410 --> 00:13:11.050
into the depths of opioid addiction despair,

00:13:11.050 --> 00:13:13.670
and thankfully, his path to recovery.

00:13:13.670 --> 00:13:15.360
His name is Dan Perez.

00:13:15.360 --> 00:13:17.250
This is a kid who grew up awkward,

00:13:17.250 --> 00:13:19.310
a kid who was obsessed with magic.

00:13:19.310 --> 00:13:21.660
He also had this gift for the written word

00:13:21.660 --> 00:13:24.170
and this very shrewd eye for culture,

00:13:24.170 --> 00:13:27.780
which were talents that prematurely catapulted him

00:13:27.780 --> 00:13:29.770
to crazy heights in the fast paced world

00:13:29.770 --> 00:13:31.370
of glossy magazine publishing,

00:13:31.370 --> 00:13:35.212
where he landed the coveted editor gig at Details Magazine

00:13:35.212 --> 00:13:39.945
all while harboring this secret debilitating dependence

