00:39:38.640 --> 00:39:40.210
into the nature of cravings,

00:39:40.210 --> 00:39:44.280
including the mechanisms and neurology behind them,

00:39:44.280 --> 00:39:45.890
as well as certain keys,

00:39:45.890 --> 00:39:48.540
including mindfulness for addressing

00:39:48.540 --> 00:39:50.960
and ultimately overcoming them.

00:39:50.960 --> 00:39:53.100
I got a ton out of this conversation

00:39:53.100 --> 00:39:54.140
and I think you will as well.

00:39:54.140 --> 00:39:55.677
So here we go.

00:39:55.677 --> 00:39:57.483
This is me and Dr. Jud Brewer.

00:39:58.770 --> 00:40:01.304
Yeah, I don't know if you've ever pulled up

00:40:01.304 --> 00:40:02.137
to a stoplight late at night,

00:40:02.137 --> 00:40:04.961
and you look around and everybody's crotch is glowing.

00:40:04.961 --> 00:40:05.794
(Jud and Rick laughing)

00:40:05.794 --> 00:40:09.970
It's like suddenly 30 seconds at a red light is intolerable.

00:40:09.970 --> 00:40:10.803
Right.

00:40:11.750 --> 00:40:12.850
Well, we only got there

00:40:12.850 --> 00:40:15.360
because we've let ourselves get there.

00:40:15.360 --> 00:40:17.470
And we can say, oh, I'm not gonna do that.

00:40:17.470 --> 00:40:21.650
I'm gonna be a good boy and willpower my way through this.

00:40:21.650 --> 00:40:25.160
Forget about it, like you said it, that doesn't work.

00:40:25.160 --> 00:40:27.150
So let's get into why it doesn't.

00:40:27.150 --> 00:40:31.070
Like, why is it that I can't override that impulse,

00:40:31.070 --> 00:40:32.950
and through sheer force of will?

00:40:32.950 --> 00:40:36.230
Like Marshall, my mental and emotional powers

00:40:36.230 --> 00:40:38.690
to prevent myself from doing that thing

00:40:38.690 --> 00:40:41.266
that I am so going to.

00:40:41.266 --> 00:40:42.570
Yeah, it sounds pretty good, doesn't it?

00:40:42.570 --> 00:40:44.280
We're these rational thinking beings.

00:40:44.280 --> 00:40:46.611
I think Descartes really sent us down a path

00:40:46.611 --> 00:40:48.860
that was not so good.

00:40:48.860 --> 00:40:49.700
Oh, I'm thinking,

00:40:49.700 --> 00:40:52.220
therefore I can think my way through stuff.

00:40:52.220 --> 00:40:53.180
It's not how our brains works.

00:40:53.180 --> 00:40:56.730
There's a part of the brain, the prefrontal cortex

00:40:56.730 --> 00:40:59.520
that's involved in willpower.

00:40:59.520 --> 00:41:01.520
It's the weakest part of the brain

00:41:01.520 --> 00:41:02.940
from an evolutionary perspective.

00:41:02.940 --> 00:41:05.790
It's the first thing that goes offline when we're stressed,

00:41:05.790 --> 00:41:09.390
when we're angry, when we're sad, when we're tired.

00:41:09.390 --> 00:41:11.820
That's why we wander into the kitchen late at night

00:41:11.820 --> 00:41:16.820
looking for something because we've learned that.

00:41:17.170 --> 00:41:18.870
And so we can say, don't do that,

00:41:18.870 --> 00:41:21.140
but then we just crash harder.

00:41:21.140 --> 00:41:24.040
And in the morning we set that resolved to like,

00:41:24.040 --> 00:41:26.720
okay, I'm really gonna do it this time.

00:41:26.720 --> 00:41:28.350
But that's just not how our brains work.

00:41:28.350 --> 00:41:30.400
Our brains don't work that way.

00:41:30.400 --> 00:41:31.470
But we think,

00:41:31.470 --> 00:41:34.480
you know, I think it's more we're rationalizing,

00:41:34.480 --> 00:41:36.760
or like, oh, willpower, it must be something,

00:41:36.760 --> 00:41:38.170
let's study it.

00:41:38.170 --> 00:41:41.120
And there's been a little bit of this and that.

00:41:41.120 --> 00:41:44.370
But it turns out that willpower, if you look at the people

00:41:44.370 --> 00:41:46.560
that "have good willpower habits,"

00:41:46.560 --> 00:41:48.510
there's some really interesting pieces there.

00:41:48.510 --> 00:41:52.710
One is they actually find things that they enjoy doing.

00:41:52.710 --> 00:41:57.160
So people do something like eat healthy or exercise.

00:41:57.160 --> 00:41:58.730
If you ask them why they do it,

00:41:58.730 --> 00:42:00.130
the people that are really good at doing it,

00:42:00.130 --> 00:42:02.013
and you probably know this personally,

00:42:02.013 --> 00:42:03.956
it feels good,

00:42:03.956 --> 00:42:08.070
as compared to, oh, I need to get in shape to get

00:42:08.070 --> 00:42:09.930
my body looking this way for the brain.

00:42:09.930 --> 00:42:12.310
It's not like an intellectual exercise.

00:42:12.310 --> 00:42:13.691
Not at all.

00:42:13.691 --> 00:42:16.470
And that, that part makes sense, but that's not willpower.

00:42:16.470 --> 00:42:19.450
And it makes sense because that is reward based learning.

00:42:19.450 --> 00:42:23.230
We're doing something out of the reward of doing it,

00:42:23.230 --> 00:42:24.127
not because we're doing it.

00:42:24.127 --> 00:42:27.390
And so that's one of the big misconceptions around willpower

00:42:27.390 --> 00:42:29.290
is that if you look at reward based learning,

00:42:29.290 --> 00:42:31.100
it's based on the reward,

00:42:31.100 --> 00:42:33.500
it's not based on the behavior itself.

00:42:33.500 --> 00:42:35.960
So if it were the behavior, we just say, stop doing this.

00:42:35.960 --> 00:42:39.400
But it's actually the reward that drives future behavior.

00:42:39.400 --> 00:42:41.750
And that's where we can start to intervene.

00:42:41.750 --> 00:42:44.890
How does it correlate with intelligence?

00:42:44.890 --> 00:42:47.840
Because just speaking from personal experience,

00:42:47.840 --> 00:42:51.190
I've noticed over the years through my adventures

00:42:51.190 --> 00:42:54.410
and journeys in the recovery community,

00:42:54.410 --> 00:42:56.850
that people who are hyper-intelligent

00:42:56.850 --> 00:42:58.560
often struggle the most

00:42:58.560 --> 00:43:01.630
because they want to intellectualize this,

00:43:01.630 --> 00:43:05.270
where truly it is an emotional thing

00:43:05.270 --> 00:43:06.380
more than anything else.

00:43:06.380 --> 00:43:10.070
And so they struggle trying to wrap their heads around

00:43:10.070 --> 00:43:11.060
how to do this,

00:43:11.060 --> 00:43:15.610
and they can't let go of the idea that that solution

00:43:15.610 --> 00:43:17.270
resides within the mind.

00:43:17.270 --> 00:43:18.564
Yeah.

00:43:18.564 --> 00:43:20.470
Well, so, hi, my name is Jud

00:43:20.470 --> 00:43:22.665
and I'm a thinking addict.

00:43:22.665 --> 00:43:24.080
There you go.

00:43:24.080 --> 00:43:27.022
If you look at the bookshelves in my house,

00:43:27.022 --> 00:43:29.164
they are way too numerous.

00:43:29.164 --> 00:43:31.470
So speaking from personal experience,

00:43:31.470 --> 00:43:33.890
and I think this applies.

00:43:33.890 --> 00:43:35.230
Is there's this,

00:43:35.230 --> 00:43:37.990
it's almost like the thinking part of our brain

00:43:37.990 --> 00:43:39.603
is kind of like this.

00:43:39.603 --> 00:43:44.380
It's like refined sugar or refined carbohydrates.

00:43:44.380 --> 00:43:46.120
It actually just gets us stoked.

00:43:46.120 --> 00:43:48.070
And we're like, oh, that's interesting.

00:43:48.070 --> 00:43:50.282
I'm just gonna learn more and I'm gonna learn more.

00:43:50.282 --> 00:43:52.320
And I'm gonna figure out the solution to this thing.

00:43:52.320 --> 00:43:54.260
Meanwhile, day after day after day,

00:43:54.260 --> 00:43:56.700
you're perpetuating the same behaviors.

00:43:56.700 --> 00:43:57.820
Unknowingly, yeah.

00:43:57.820 --> 00:44:01.630
While you're buying every self-help book that's available.

00:44:01.630 --> 00:44:02.620
Totally.

00:44:02.620 --> 00:44:05.360
So what we really need is to land in our body,

00:44:05.360 --> 00:44:08.090
'cause our body is really, really wise.

00:44:08.090 --> 00:44:10.320
And so, this is this intellectual thing,

00:44:10.320 --> 00:44:14.120
is like it's just drives more addiction.

00:44:14.120 --> 00:44:15.690
Whereas like, I wanna learn more.

00:44:15.690 --> 00:44:17.930
As compared to really landing on our direct experience

00:44:17.930 --> 00:44:20.573
that says, dude, why would you do that?

00:44:20.573 --> 00:44:22.590
I'll give you an example.

00:44:22.590 --> 00:44:25.300
So, we did a study

00:44:25.300 --> 00:44:29.120
with people who are trying to quit smoking.

00:44:29.120 --> 00:44:32.760
And we randomize people to cognitive therapy

00:44:32.760 --> 00:44:33.910
or mindfulness training,

00:44:33.910 --> 00:44:36.080
where we train them to really just pay attention

00:44:36.080 --> 00:44:38.170
to the results of their behavior.

00:44:38.170 --> 00:44:40.230
So when they come into the mindfulness group,

00:44:40.230 --> 00:44:41.810
they don't even know what they're getting.

00:44:41.810 --> 00:44:43.739
And so they come in and they're like,

00:44:43.739 --> 00:44:44.572
I'm here to quit smoking.

00:44:44.572 --> 00:44:45.460
And I say, okay.

00:44:45.460 --> 00:44:48.110
Next, when you go home, smoke.

00:44:48.110 --> 00:44:49.340
And they're looking at me like,

00:44:49.340 --> 00:44:51.410
is this the experiment that you're running?

00:44:51.410 --> 00:44:52.690
Is this the study?

00:44:52.690 --> 00:44:56.550
And I say, no, smoke but pay attention as you smoke

00:44:56.550 --> 00:44:57.570
and see what happens.

00:44:57.570 --> 00:45:00.470
So they pay attention to the smell, to the taste,

00:45:00.470 --> 00:45:02.200
to the feeling of the superheated smoke

00:45:02.200 --> 00:45:03.540
going into their lungs.

00:45:03.540 --> 00:45:06.560
And they come back and there's this Mr. York look

00:45:06.560 --> 00:45:07.824
on their face.

00:45:07.824 --> 00:45:08.957
They're like, oh my God,

00:45:08.957 --> 00:45:10.330
how that I never noticed that before?

00:45:10.330 --> 00:45:13.460
'Cause they realized that smoking tastes like shit.

00:45:13.460 --> 00:45:16.890
And they can only get that wisdom

00:45:16.890 --> 00:45:18.073
from their direct experience.

00:45:18.073 --> 00:45:19.570
I had a guy who...

00:45:19.570 --> 00:45:21.123
So we, in our first study,

00:45:22.367 --> 00:45:25.160
first class was on Monday, second class was on Thursday.

00:45:25.160 --> 00:45:27.540
This guy was smoking 30 cigarettes a day.

00:45:27.540 --> 00:45:29.100
He been smoking that for a long time.

00:45:29.100 --> 00:45:30.367
He came back on Thursday and he said,

00:45:30.367 --> 00:45:32.520
"Yeah, I'm down to 10 cigarettes."

00:45:32.520 --> 00:45:33.680
And I said, "Well, what happened?"

00:45:33.680 --> 00:45:36.690
And he said, "Well, I noticed that I would drink coffee

00:45:36.690 --> 00:45:37.900
and the coffee was kind of bitter,

00:45:37.900 --> 00:45:40.170
so I'd smoke a cigarette to numb myself from the taste

00:45:40.170 --> 00:45:42.950
'cause it's amazing how smoking numbs your taste."

00:45:42.950 --> 00:45:45.180
And so he realized, well, I don't need to smoke,

00:45:45.180 --> 00:45:46.490
I could brush my teeth.

00:45:46.490 --> 00:45:48.640
And he just went through this litany of 20 cigarettes

00:45:48.640 --> 00:45:51.690
where he was smoking all these things out of habit,

00:45:51.690 --> 00:45:53.020
where he'd learned

00:45:53.962 --> 00:45:55.550
through this reward based learning process that,

00:45:55.550 --> 00:45:58.646
oh, if I smoke, I feel better, you know, or whatever.

00:45:58.646 --> 00:46:00.940
And he realized, oh, this is not a good way to go.

00:46:00.940 --> 00:46:04.070
So the idea being, you talk about this in your book,

00:46:04.070 --> 00:46:07.480
is diverging from what Skinner calls

00:46:07.480 --> 00:46:09.870
the operant conditioning, right?

00:46:09.870 --> 00:46:12.640
Which has behaviorism, this traditional approach

00:46:12.640 --> 00:46:14.830
to like dealing with these kinds of problems,

00:46:14.830 --> 00:46:17.060
to a more Buddhist perspective,

00:46:17.060 --> 00:46:21.050
which is called dependent origination, right?

00:46:21.050 --> 00:46:24.323
And this involves being present for the experience,

00:46:25.210 --> 00:46:29.040
rather than getting into judgment, self judgment,

00:46:29.040 --> 00:46:32.290
to just be curious about what's happening.

00:46:32.290 --> 00:46:33.610
Yeah, absolutely.

00:46:33.610 --> 00:46:34.880
And it's interesting.

00:46:34.880 --> 00:46:39.880
So one of the first aha moments for us in my research career

00:46:40.920 --> 00:46:41.960
was when I was looking,

00:46:41.960 --> 00:46:43.980
I was studying this operant conditioning

00:46:43.980 --> 00:46:46.290
or positive negative reinforcement.

00:46:46.290 --> 00:46:47.590
And I was thinking, wait a minute,

00:46:47.590 --> 00:46:49.770
this sounds way too familiar.

00:46:49.770 --> 00:46:53.131
And I started looking into this because I've learned,

00:46:53.131 --> 00:46:54.720
on a retreat or something,

00:46:54.720 --> 00:46:57.540
I'd learned this dependent origination piece.

00:46:57.540 --> 00:46:59.754
And it was kind of complex,

00:46:59.754 --> 00:47:01.630
there are these 12 Steps and all this stuff about birth,

00:47:01.630 --> 00:47:03.800
and this, and I was like, well, what is this?

00:47:03.800 --> 00:47:04.990
But when I looked at it,

00:47:04.990 --> 00:47:07.310
and I actually worked with a poly scholar

00:47:07.310 --> 00:47:08.480
to really explore this,

00:47:08.480 --> 00:47:11.640
it turns out that dependent origination

00:47:11.640 --> 00:47:14.150
explains operant conditioning.

00:47:14.150 --> 00:47:18.000
And so the Buddhist psychologists had figured this out

00:47:18.000 --> 00:47:21.960
2,500 years ago, before paper was even invented.

00:47:21.960 --> 00:47:24.380
And so they were describing the same process.

00:47:24.380 --> 00:47:28.440
And importantly, this process, dependent origination,

00:47:28.440 --> 00:47:31.210
was what reportedly the Buddha was contemplating

00:47:31.210 --> 00:47:32.830
on the night of his enlightenment.

00:47:32.830 --> 00:47:36.770
As in, hey, pay attention guys, this is kind of important.

00:47:36.770 --> 00:47:38.590
So, a really important concept

00:47:38.590 --> 00:47:41.560
that actually is rediscovered in modern day

00:47:41.560 --> 00:47:44.230
and drives and explains

00:47:44.230 --> 00:47:46.493
a lot of how addictive behavior is formed.

00:47:48.270 --> 00:47:49.760
Science and ancient wisdom are great.

00:47:49.760 --> 00:47:52.080
They both explain a lot of course,

00:47:52.080 --> 00:47:54.280
about how addictive behaviors are formed.

00:47:54.280 --> 00:47:58.580
But the scary thing is the drive towards compulsion

00:47:58.580 --> 00:48:01.100
is part of what it means to be human.

00:48:01.100 --> 00:48:05.930
It's literally baked into our physiology and neurochemistry.

00:48:05.930 --> 00:48:09.690
Which means, addiction can happen to anyone.

00:48:09.690 --> 00:48:13.360
And that is the message of my next guest, Amy Dresner.

00:48:13.360 --> 00:48:16.800
Amy is a former stand-up comic, a recovering drug addict,

00:48:16.800 --> 00:48:19.990
and all around fuck up; her words, not mine,

00:48:19.990 --> 00:48:22.880
as well as a writer and an author

00:48:22.880 --> 00:48:26.310
who humorously chronicles are epic ups and downs

00:48:26.310 --> 00:48:31.290
for a variety of outlets from psychology today, to salon.

00:48:31.290 --> 00:48:33.949
I love a good addiction recovery yarn, and Amy's first book,

00:48:33.949 --> 00:48:37.880
"My Fair Junkie" is a completely wild ride,

00:48:37.880 --> 00:48:40.700
that chronicles her downward spiral

00:48:40.700 --> 00:48:43.580
from someone who pretty much had it all growing up,

00:48:43.580 --> 00:48:47.380
to becoming this addiction monster;

00:48:47.380 --> 00:48:52.380
meth, Oxycontin, alcohol, and sex among her drugs of choice.

00:48:52.960 --> 00:48:56.060
It's a journey that led to felony conviction

00:48:56.060 --> 00:48:59.150
for domestic abuse, psych wards, penniless,

00:48:59.150 --> 00:49:01.840
a community service chain gang,

00:49:01.840 --> 00:49:04.900
and pretty much more rehabs and halfway houses

00:49:04.900 --> 00:49:08.410
than you can count, until she finally gets sober

00:49:08.410 --> 00:49:12.703
and has to face completely starting over in her 40s.

00:49:13.670 --> 00:49:16.870
Yes, addiction can happen to anyone.

00:49:16.870 --> 00:49:21.870
But that also means that recovery can happen for anyone.

00:49:22.040 --> 00:49:26.050
And as Amy likes to say, if she can get sober,

00:49:26.050 --> 00:49:27.700
anyone can get sober.

00:49:27.700 --> 00:49:30.940
So here's an excerpt from episode 428,

00:49:30.940 --> 00:49:32.633
with my friend, Amy Dresner.

00:49:34.340 --> 00:49:36.060
I have people on that are in recovery.

00:49:36.060 --> 00:49:38.430
Like I always wanna hear what they have to say

00:49:38.430 --> 00:49:42.110
to somebody who might be listening, who is struggling.

00:49:42.110 --> 00:49:45.970
Whether it's with relationships, sex, or substances,

00:49:45.970 --> 00:49:49.780
or some other behavior that is causing them pain,

00:49:49.780 --> 00:49:52.210
as somebody who's been there and found their way out,

00:49:52.210 --> 00:49:54.500
like, how do you speak to that person?

00:49:54.500 --> 00:49:58.113
I would say, first of all, be gentle with yourself.

00:50:00.570 --> 00:50:03.807
Drop the shame because that will just continue use.

00:50:03.807 --> 00:50:06.260
Like you're doing the best you can

00:50:06.260 --> 00:50:08.305
with the tools that you have.

00:50:08.305 --> 00:50:13.305
I would say to be honest with other people and get help

00:50:13.970 --> 00:50:15.640
and go get a therapist,

00:50:15.640 --> 00:50:18.230
or go to AA or go to slower or whatever,

00:50:18.230 --> 00:50:21.360
get into a support group, because it's so important.

00:50:21.360 --> 00:50:26.360
When we're in a behavior that feels pathological,

00:50:27.210 --> 00:50:30.280
we isolate and that just makes it so much worse.

00:50:30.280 --> 00:50:32.240
And for me, a huge part of the healing

00:50:32.240 --> 00:50:35.760
has been the fellowship and my friends

00:50:35.760 --> 00:50:38.593
and feeling connected and reaching out.

00:50:39.450 --> 00:50:40.500
I would say, don't give up,

00:50:40.500 --> 00:50:42.307
no matter how many times you slip.

00:50:42.307 --> 00:50:45.433
Like if you're alive, you've still got a chance.

00:50:46.828 --> 00:50:48.740
That you can get through this.

00:50:48.740 --> 00:50:50.300
That you're not a bad person,

00:50:50.300 --> 00:50:53.308
even if you've done bad things.

00:50:53.308 --> 00:50:55.600
I mean, I think that's amazing advice.

00:50:55.600 --> 00:50:57.600
I think the hardest part for people

00:50:59.609 --> 00:51:01.450
is that first step.

00:51:01.450 --> 00:51:02.940
Like, well, how do I reach out?

00:51:02.940 --> 00:51:04.323
Or like,

00:51:04.323 --> 00:51:08.299
what is the thing that I actually need to do first?

00:51:08.299 --> 00:51:09.505
And the other thing is,

00:51:09.505 --> 00:51:12.130
don't wait till you feel like you're ready.

00:51:12.130 --> 00:51:13.100
You'll wait forever.

00:51:13.100 --> 00:51:15.570
Like, if I waited until I was ready to write a book,

00:51:15.570 --> 00:51:18.040
or if I like I've been waiting to feel like I'm ready

00:51:18.040 --> 00:51:19.200
and wanna go to the gym,

00:51:19.200 --> 00:51:21.060
and that's been a year and a half right now.

00:51:21.060 --> 00:51:22.100
Don't judge me.

00:51:22.100 --> 00:51:22.933
But it's like,

00:51:22.933 --> 00:51:27.670
you take action and that changes the feeling.

00:51:27.670 --> 00:51:29.320
Mood follows action.

00:51:29.320 --> 00:51:32.390
That took me forever to figure out.

00:51:32.390 --> 00:51:35.427
And there's a line in the book that my dad told me.

00:51:35.427 --> 00:51:37.860
And it took me 20 years to figure it out.

00:51:37.860 --> 00:51:42.210
He said, "Stability doesn't create discipline,

00:51:42.210 --> 00:51:44.590
discipline creates stability."

00:51:44.590 --> 00:51:49.310
I was waiting to feel okay before I could do these things,

00:51:49.310 --> 00:51:53.600
but it was doing these things that made me feel okay.

00:51:53.600 --> 00:51:55.200
So I would say,

00:51:55.200 --> 00:51:57.380
tell your feelings to shut the fuck up,

00:51:57.380 --> 00:51:59.010
your head to shut the fuck up,

00:51:59.010 --> 00:52:02.090
and you take the action and it will change things, you know?

00:52:02.090 --> 00:52:04.550
And it's like, you can't get better.

00:52:04.550 --> 00:52:06.130
If I can get sober, anyone can get sober.

00:52:06.130 --> 00:52:07.678
Sorry, like, come on.

00:52:07.678 --> 00:52:09.543
That's where it's gnarly.

00:52:11.300 --> 00:52:14.020
And one of the things you've written a lot about

00:52:14.020 --> 00:52:16.230
is the importance of structure in your life.

00:52:16.230 --> 00:52:17.230
Oh God, yeah.

00:52:17.230 --> 00:52:18.850
Well now, I mean, I work from home.

00:52:18.850 --> 00:52:22.703
I work from home.

00:52:22.703 --> 00:52:25.570
I have a three day a week editing job.

00:52:25.570 --> 00:52:26.790
I work for...

00:52:26.790 --> 00:52:28.610
And it's hard.

00:52:28.610 --> 00:52:29.773
It's really hard.

00:52:31.540 --> 00:52:35.553
I have to force myself to like get out and do things.

00:52:37.102 --> 00:52:40.220
I guess the other thing too, it's like, you can change,

00:52:40.220 --> 00:52:41.433
you can change.

00:52:42.470 --> 00:52:43.990
I really thought forever I was broken,

00:52:43.990 --> 00:52:46.100
and I was stuck with that person who I was.

00:52:46.100 --> 00:52:50.060
And it's like, I'm not that person today.

00:52:50.060 --> 00:52:51.490
I'm a completely different person.

00:52:51.490 --> 00:52:54.380
And it's like, I never thought that I could be

00:52:54.380 --> 00:52:58.420
the person who shows up and is inspiring to people

00:52:58.420 --> 00:53:02.810
and sweeps the floor, not the in streets,

00:53:02.810 --> 00:53:03.643
but you know what I mean?

00:53:03.643 --> 00:53:05.207
Like it's...

00:53:05.207 --> 00:53:06.700
Yeah, and when you're in that cycle

00:53:06.700 --> 00:53:09.130
and you're surrounded by people who are telling you,

00:53:09.130 --> 00:53:11.130
you're a piece of and you're never gonna change.

00:53:11.130 --> 00:53:12.481
Yeah.

00:53:12.481 --> 00:53:13.314
Of course, yeah.

00:53:13.314 --> 00:53:14.980
Fuck what people tell you.

00:53:14.980 --> 00:53:15.813
Yeah.

00:53:15.813 --> 00:53:18.560
Yeah, fuck what people tell you, you can do it.

00:53:18.560 --> 00:53:22.040
And also the other thing too is like,

00:53:22.040 --> 00:53:25.990
if you have an urge to use or drink or whatever,

00:53:25.990 --> 00:53:27.910
or text that guy that's bad for you or whatever,

00:53:27.910 --> 00:53:31.130
it's like, give yourself 20 minutes.

00:53:31.130 --> 00:53:34.760
Take a bath, call someone, watch an episode of "Ozark,"

00:53:34.760 --> 00:53:36.080
jack off, whatever it is.

00:53:36.080 --> 00:53:38.470
Like, do something and buy yourself 20 minutes.

00:53:38.470 --> 00:53:41.421
Call someone, take a drive, take a walk.

00:53:41.421 --> 00:53:45.443
Because the urge passes, whether you pick up or not.

00:53:47.110 --> 00:53:49.100
It took me a long time to figure that out.

00:53:49.100 --> 00:53:51.530
And it's like, but if you don't ever sort of wait

00:53:51.530 --> 00:53:52.950
through that feeling,

00:53:52.950 --> 00:53:54.910
you don't realize that you can get through it.

00:53:54.910 --> 00:53:57.120
It feels overwhelming, and then the urge comes to you,

00:53:57.120 --> 00:53:59.330
like I gotta do what, I gotta do it, you know?

00:53:59.330 --> 00:54:00.650
Yeah, and you feel like you're gonna die.

00:54:00.650 --> 00:54:01.763
If you don't do it.

00:54:01.763 --> 00:54:05.620
And you don't understand that it's so true.

00:54:05.620 --> 00:54:07.733
Like it will pass.

00:54:07.733 --> 00:54:08.636
Yes.

00:54:08.636 --> 00:54:10.740
And if you could just get through that

00:54:10.740 --> 00:54:12.503
and realize and go, oh,

00:54:13.750 --> 00:54:15.930
you realize that the feeling, doesn't control you,

00:54:15.930 --> 00:54:19.290
it won't kill you, you don't have to obey it.

00:54:19.290 --> 00:54:21.500
And that's where that freedom comes from.

00:54:21.500 --> 00:54:24.880
You're like, oh, I can change.

00:54:24.880 --> 00:54:26.060
And it's just slow.

00:54:26.060 --> 00:54:27.805
It's a slow process as you know.

00:54:27.805 --> 00:54:31.100
Takes a while to slowberity.

00:54:31.100 --> 00:54:32.553
I know, right.

00:54:32.553 --> 00:54:33.656
I hate that.

00:54:33.656 --> 00:54:35.880
The all annoying slogans.

00:54:35.880 --> 00:54:38.720
But that you can rewire your brain slowly over time,

00:54:38.720 --> 00:54:39.800
you know, through action.

00:54:39.800 --> 00:54:41.120
You really can.

00:54:41.120 --> 00:54:42.590
And you can be happy.

00:54:42.590 --> 00:54:44.520
You can have a good life and you don't have to be

00:54:44.520 --> 00:54:48.470
a prisoner to that crap, you know?

00:54:48.470 --> 00:54:50.740
And just also don't care what people in the room are saying.

00:54:50.740 --> 00:54:52.941
Like if you eat it and just come back,

00:54:52.941 --> 00:54:54.810
it's not a competition.

00:54:54.810 --> 00:54:56.090
I don't like that.

00:54:56.090 --> 00:54:57.730
Everyone's got their own stuff.

00:54:57.730 --> 00:54:58.840
Everyone's got their own bag.

00:54:58.840 --> 00:55:00.940
And some people have like really severe mental illness.

00:55:00.940 --> 00:55:04.580
Like everyone has their own thing, you know?

00:55:04.580 --> 00:55:06.480
Yeah, I think that keeps a lot of people away.

00:55:06.480 --> 00:55:09.890
They come in and they have a weird exchange with somebody

00:55:09.890 --> 00:55:12.180
and then they're like, I'm done with that.

00:55:12.180 --> 00:55:13.510
And then they're dead,

00:55:13.510 --> 00:55:15.970
or they can't get sober.

00:55:15.970 --> 00:55:17.317
Yeah.

00:55:17.317 --> 00:55:18.150
It's like, you know, find another meeting,

00:55:18.150 --> 00:55:19.190
or it's like, you know,

00:55:19.190 --> 00:55:21.050
I mean, I just wrote a piece about this

00:55:21.050 --> 00:55:23.910
in this Dr. Howard Wetsman said 50% of people

00:55:28.325 --> 00:55:31.238
who relapse will never go back to it.

00:55:31.238 --> 00:55:32.980
That's heartbreaking.

00:55:32.980 --> 00:55:34.368
Yeah.

00:55:34.368 --> 00:55:35.267
Because either someone said something to them

00:55:35.267 --> 00:55:37.670
or because of shame, not that they, yeah.

00:55:37.670 --> 00:55:38.530
That 50% gets sober,

00:55:38.530 --> 00:55:40.350
but they will never even make a second try.

00:55:40.350 --> 00:55:43.140
And it was like, you know, I have such a big dose of like,

00:55:43.140 --> 00:55:44.953
who gives a fuck about me?

00:55:44.953 --> 00:55:47.670
Like, I was never, like, I gotta be queen of the drunks.

00:55:47.670 --> 00:55:49.040
Like I was just like, I don't care.

00:55:49.040 --> 00:55:51.430
Like this is my life, and I don't care

00:55:51.430 --> 00:55:52.390
what these people think.

00:55:52.390 --> 00:55:53.930
And my social capital is not based

00:55:53.930 --> 00:55:55.880
on what people in AA think about me.

00:55:55.880 --> 00:55:59.060
And I was just like, I'm on my own trajectory.

00:55:59.060 --> 00:56:00.220
Well, that's how you get well.

00:56:00.220 --> 00:56:02.863
Because you can't save your ass.

00:56:02.863 --> 00:56:05.170
If you're trying to save face and that good,

00:56:05.170 --> 00:56:07.232
then you're working across purposely.

00:56:07.232 --> 00:56:09.970
Exactly, exactly, exactly.

00:56:09.970 --> 00:56:11.552
It's like, it's not a competition.

00:56:11.552 --> 00:56:12.663
You know?

00:56:15.250 --> 00:56:19.800
Okay, so as we step into the back half of this episode,

00:56:19.800 --> 00:56:23.024
I wanna turn our attention to another powerful story

00:56:23.024 --> 00:56:24.943
of from opioid abuse.

00:56:25.988 --> 00:56:29.080
But this time I wanna zero in on that moment,

00:56:29.080 --> 00:56:32.890
that specific moment when an addict knows

00:56:32.890 --> 00:56:35.040
they're ready for a recovery.

00:56:35.040 --> 00:56:36.950
It's a story that highlights the gift

00:56:36.950 --> 00:56:39.040
that hitting rock bottom can be,

00:56:39.040 --> 00:56:42.340
how one goes about piecing their life back together

00:56:42.340 --> 00:56:43.380
after losing everything,

00:56:43.380 --> 00:56:46.800
and ultimately about how AA save lives.

00:56:46.800 --> 00:56:50.140
I'm talking about the story of Jeff Grants.

00:56:50.140 --> 00:56:52.130
Jeff is a former New York City attorney

00:56:52.130 --> 00:56:54.380
who became addicted to painkillers

00:56:54.380 --> 00:56:56.570
in the wake of a ruptured achilles

00:56:56.570 --> 00:56:58.740
he suffered playing basketball.

00:56:58.740 --> 00:57:01.800
It didn't take long before he started making bad decisions

00:57:01.800 --> 00:57:05.350
under the influence, fueled by this dependency

00:57:05.350 --> 00:57:09.410
that led to let's call them ethical transgressions,

00:57:09.410 --> 00:57:11.417
as well as financial misdeeds,

00:57:11.417 --> 00:57:16.300
losing control of his law firm, and even a suicide attempt.

00:57:16.300 --> 00:57:17.580
Jeff survives all of this.

00:57:17.580 --> 00:57:20.140
He even enters treatment, gets sober,

00:57:20.140 --> 00:57:23.050
starts to put the pieces of his life back together.

00:57:23.050 --> 00:57:26.980
But then at about a year and a half into sobriety,

00:57:26.980 --> 00:57:29.150
he gets arrested for fraud.

00:57:29.150 --> 00:57:32.450
He pleads guilty and ends up serving 18 months

00:57:32.450 --> 00:57:34.330
in federal prison.

00:57:34.330 --> 00:57:38.240
Today, Jeff is a pastor serving the imprisoned

00:57:38.240 --> 00:57:40.320
as well as the underprivileged,

00:57:40.320 --> 00:57:43.220
who is also now more than 15 years sober.

00:57:43.220 --> 00:57:45.940
So this is his story of redemption

00:57:45.940 --> 00:57:49.950
and how 12 Step programs give people hope.

00:57:49.950 --> 00:57:51.653
Here is Jeff Grants.

00:57:53.160 --> 00:57:55.412
So I said to him,

00:57:55.412 --> 00:58:00.412
all right, why don't you just resign my law license for me?

00:58:01.250 --> 00:58:03.580
And I went to my doctor friend and I got

00:58:03.580 --> 00:58:08.580
a prescription of Demerol, 40 tabs and went home.

00:58:10.340 --> 00:58:14.790
And after my ex-wife and kids went to sleep,

00:58:14.790 --> 00:58:17.050
I took the whole bile.

00:58:17.050 --> 00:58:18.740
It's like 40 tabs.

00:58:18.740 --> 00:58:21.258
Yeah, yeah.

00:58:21.258 --> 00:58:23.400
And yeah, I knew what I was doing.

00:58:23.400 --> 00:58:25.432
I mean, I was trying to kill myself.

00:58:25.432 --> 00:58:28.210
Was it a real suicide attempt

00:58:28.210 --> 00:58:30.113
or was it a desperate call for help?

00:58:31.607 --> 00:58:32.840
I mean, 40 seems like enough.

00:58:32.840 --> 00:58:35.300
But your tolerance must've been insane.

00:58:35.300 --> 00:58:37.730
My tolerance was insane, but I don't think...

00:58:39.390 --> 00:58:41.130
I thought 40 would be enough.

00:58:41.130 --> 00:58:42.110
That was the point.

00:58:42.110 --> 00:58:43.740
I thought 40 would be enough.

00:58:43.740 --> 00:58:46.727
And I've spent a lot of years trying to figure out

00:58:46.727 --> 00:58:51.727
whether or not it was a real attempt or a cry for help.

00:58:51.980 --> 00:58:54.833
But I wanted the noise in my head to stop.

00:58:58.301 --> 00:59:00.313
But a few days later,

00:59:02.393 --> 00:59:06.293
I called the Silver Hill Hospital in New Canaan

00:59:06.293 --> 00:59:08.320
and I wanted to go there.

00:59:08.320 --> 00:59:11.293
I knew to go there because clients of mine who had

00:59:11.293 --> 00:59:13.700
(indistinct)

00:59:13.700 --> 00:59:16.400
So you made a decision like I'm done,

00:59:16.400 --> 00:59:20.470
and you did your own self style detox at home.

00:59:20.470 --> 00:59:23.500
Well, I had done it 100 times before,

00:59:23.500 --> 00:59:26.470
so it wasn't as if I was ignorant

00:59:26.470 --> 00:59:27.870
of what a withdrawal would be like.

00:59:27.870 --> 00:59:29.550
This was just the super one.

00:59:29.550 --> 00:59:31.720
This was the biggest one I'd ever been through.

00:59:31.720 --> 00:59:33.763
And certainly the first one

00:59:33.763 --> 00:59:36.470
that I overtly tried to kill myself.

00:59:36.470 --> 00:59:38.340
Opiate withdrawal is gnarly.

00:59:38.340 --> 00:59:41.529
It was bad, it was bad.

00:59:41.529 --> 00:59:43.501
How long did it go on for?

00:59:43.501 --> 00:59:46.190
I don't know, three or four days, probably.

00:59:46.190 --> 00:59:47.023
It was bad.

00:59:47.023 --> 00:59:49.660
So you waited until you weathered that

00:59:49.660 --> 00:59:53.410
before you checked yourself into treatment?

00:59:53.410 --> 00:59:54.820
Oh yeah,

00:59:54.820 --> 00:59:57.660
because I knew better than anybody.

00:59:57.660 --> 00:59:58.747
You did.

00:59:58.747 --> 01:00:00.030
So you had a sense that this...

01:00:00.030 --> 01:00:03.510
As somebody who's tried to get sober many times on your own,

01:00:03.510 --> 01:00:04.343
you know what it's like.

01:00:04.343 --> 01:00:06.420
It's like, okay, this time, I'm never gonna do it again,

01:00:06.420 --> 01:00:09.070
but kind of in the back of your head, you're like,

01:00:09.070 --> 01:00:10.860
you're not really sold on the idea,

01:00:10.860 --> 01:00:13.340
but this was qualitatively different, you knew.

01:00:13.340 --> 01:00:14.650
Well, I didn't have a life to go back to.

01:00:14.650 --> 01:00:15.870
I knew that.

01:00:15.870 --> 01:00:17.541
I knew everything was gone.

01:00:17.541 --> 01:00:20.905
I didn't really understand at all at that point

01:00:20.905 --> 01:00:23.134
the gift of a hard bottom.

01:00:23.134 --> 01:00:25.940
That's something I learned later.

01:00:25.940 --> 01:00:27.930
But everything was gone.

01:00:27.930 --> 01:00:32.497
And so going to rehab,

01:00:32.497 --> 01:00:34.230
it was a blessing.

01:00:34.230 --> 01:00:37.930
So how do you begin to piece your life back together

01:00:37.930 --> 01:00:40.010
in the aftermath of all of this?

01:00:40.010 --> 01:00:43.430
Well, certainly through recovery.

01:00:43.430 --> 01:00:44.263
I mean,

01:00:45.860 --> 01:00:49.671
seven weeks in rehab and they brought recovery meetings in,

01:00:49.671 --> 01:00:52.420
and I kind of took to them.

01:00:52.420 --> 01:00:57.420
And more, I think to the structure than to anything,

01:01:00.270 --> 01:01:03.403
I was too out of it to learn anything, I was gone.

01:01:04.270 --> 01:01:08.650
And the first day out of rehab,

01:01:08.650 --> 01:01:10.560
I did what I was told to do.

01:01:10.560 --> 01:01:13.307
I showed up at the meeting and I raised my hand and I said,

01:01:13.307 --> 01:01:15.740
"I'm Jeff and I'm an alcoholic and a drug addict.

01:01:15.740 --> 01:01:17.909
And I needed temporary sponsor."

01:01:17.909 --> 01:01:22.486
And I gave like three seconds

01:01:22.486 --> 01:01:26.380
on my drug of choice, or I can't remember right now.

01:01:26.380 --> 01:01:28.410
But at the end of the meeting,

01:01:28.410 --> 01:01:30.770
the leader who had been leading the meeting,

01:01:30.770 --> 01:01:33.650
and I thought it was the boss,

01:01:33.650 --> 01:01:35.770
I didn't know, that was my first meeting.

01:01:35.770 --> 01:01:36.850
I didn't know.

01:01:36.850 --> 01:01:39.433
And he came up to me and he said,

01:01:41.217 --> 01:01:43.800
"This is for alcoholics and you've done drugs

01:01:43.800 --> 01:01:45.450
and you're in the wrong meeting."

01:01:46.780 --> 01:01:48.770
How dare that guy say that?

01:01:48.770 --> 01:01:51.750
I've never heard anybody say anything like that.

01:01:51.750 --> 01:01:52.710
You're a West Coast guy.

01:01:52.710 --> 01:01:54.330
Yeah, that's interesting.

01:01:54.330 --> 01:01:55.750
Wow!

01:01:55.750 --> 01:01:56.583
And this other guy came up to me,

01:01:56.583 --> 01:01:58.342
who was standing right there

01:01:58.342 --> 01:01:59.920
and he was the spitting image of Freddie Mercury.

01:01:59.920 --> 01:02:02.920
I am telling you, this guy, I thought it was Freddie Mercury

01:02:02.920 --> 01:02:04.430
in my haze.

01:02:04.430 --> 01:02:06.440
And he said to me,

01:02:06.440 --> 01:02:09.280
"Don't mind that guy.

01:02:09.280 --> 01:02:11.610
I'll be your temporary sponsor."

01:02:11.610 --> 01:02:15.470
And Brian T was my sponsor.

01:02:15.470 --> 01:02:18.490
And he gave me very clear instructions.

01:02:18.490 --> 01:02:19.323
Like,

01:02:20.940 --> 01:02:21.773
what to do.

01:02:21.773 --> 01:02:25.800
And for 30, 60 days, I went to a noon meeting every day

01:02:26.820 --> 01:02:28.170
and I fell asleep

01:02:30.390 --> 01:02:33.540
with my head against the wall 'cause I couldn't even focus.

01:02:33.540 --> 01:02:35.667
But you showed up, took direction,

01:02:35.667 --> 01:02:37.360
made yourself known.

01:02:37.360 --> 01:02:38.680
Yep.

01:02:38.680 --> 01:02:40.380
And began to take accountability.

01:02:40.380 --> 01:02:44.460
Did all that stuff that seems completely unrelated

01:02:44.460 --> 01:02:45.820
to staying sober,

01:02:45.820 --> 01:02:49.236
like, three people, take numbers, make coffee.

01:02:49.236 --> 01:02:52.870
Lie a lie.

01:02:52.870 --> 01:02:55.300
Keep coming back.

01:02:55.300 --> 01:02:56.363
Yeah, exactly.

01:02:58.644 --> 01:03:01.088
But we had to get rid of the house.

01:03:01.088 --> 01:03:03.490
And of course, I did whatever.

01:03:03.490 --> 01:03:05.543
You have no income at this point.

01:03:06.460 --> 01:03:07.730
No income at this point.

01:03:07.730 --> 01:03:11.440
And a little, some savings, but no income.

01:03:11.440 --> 01:03:15.190
And I did what every same guy does

01:03:15.190 --> 01:03:17.680
when they lose their house and their career

01:03:17.680 --> 01:03:20.090
and their reputation.

01:03:20.090 --> 01:03:21.845
I moved to Greenwich,

01:03:21.845 --> 01:03:24.530
one of the wealthiest communities in the country.

01:03:24.530 --> 01:03:25.810
Why would you do that?

01:03:26.677 --> 01:03:29.440
Because I had started going to AA meetings there,

01:03:29.440 --> 01:03:31.140
the recovery meetings there.

01:03:31.140 --> 01:03:36.010
And those meetings were so important to me

01:03:36.010 --> 01:03:37.530
that I had to be there.

01:03:37.530 --> 01:03:41.100
And also, it was only six miles from our home.

01:03:41.100 --> 01:03:45.100
And although the state line is huge in terms of media

01:03:45.100 --> 01:03:48.090
and in terms of interconnectivity.

01:03:48.090 --> 01:03:50.130
But I figured my kids would still be able

01:03:50.130 --> 01:03:53.090
to maintain relationships with their friends.

01:03:53.090 --> 01:03:55.790
And that was true, that happened.

01:03:55.790 --> 01:03:57.413
But there we were in Greenwich,

01:03:59.488 --> 01:04:03.045
and for the next 20 months or so,

01:04:03.045 --> 01:04:05.783
I was living in an apartment in Greenwich

01:04:05.783 --> 01:04:06.698
and going to meetings.

01:04:06.698 --> 01:04:09.882
And I went three times a day, four times a day sometimes.

01:04:09.882 --> 01:04:14.120
And I was a lock step in recovery.

01:04:14.120 --> 01:04:15.568
That was my life.

01:04:15.568 --> 01:04:18.000
And this saves your life.

01:04:18.000 --> 01:04:18.833
Saved my life.

01:04:18.833 --> 01:04:20.160
Yeah, absolutely.

01:04:20.160 --> 01:04:23.070
It's interesting to me, I'm always encouraged

01:04:23.070 --> 01:04:26.571
when I hear stories like yours of people

01:04:26.571 --> 01:04:30.060
whose lives have been spared as a result of the 12 Steps,

01:04:30.060 --> 01:04:33.790
because in our fast paced modern culture,

01:04:33.790 --> 01:04:36.350
it seems like every year there's some new hot take

01:04:36.350 --> 01:04:39.240
on what sobriety is or should be.

01:04:39.240 --> 01:04:42.210
And now we know more about addiction and alcoholism

01:04:42.210 --> 01:04:43.490
than we ever have before.

01:04:43.490 --> 01:04:46.909
And all these other ideas are antiquated.

01:04:46.909 --> 01:04:50.070
And maybe there's truth in that, maybe there's not.

01:04:50.070 --> 01:04:53.230
But I know that 12 Step and Alcoholics Anonymous

01:04:53.230 --> 01:04:54.063
saved my life.

01:04:54.956 --> 01:04:56.610
It's saved the lives of so many people that I know.

01:04:56.610 --> 01:04:57.583
And it works.

01:04:59.796 --> 01:05:01.309
It works if you're...

01:05:01.309 --> 01:05:02.520
It worked for me.

01:05:02.520 --> 01:05:05.390
I have no opinion on how people get sober.

01:05:05.390 --> 01:05:08.100
If they find other ways of doing that, more power to them.

01:05:08.100 --> 01:05:10.160
I just know that this is what has worked for me

01:05:10.160 --> 01:05:11.980
and continues to work for me

01:05:11.980 --> 01:05:14.470
and remains my number one priority.

01:05:14.470 --> 01:05:15.303
It's a big world.

01:05:15.303 --> 01:05:18.170
And I assume there's people getting sober other ways.

01:05:18.170 --> 01:05:20.763
For me, it worked.

01:05:21.653 --> 01:05:24.450
And it gave me a home, you know, a family,

01:05:24.450 --> 01:05:26.923
people who weren't judging me.

01:05:28.950 --> 01:05:33.950
And I had abandoned all my people, places and things

01:05:34.370 --> 01:05:36.790
because nobody would talk to me.

01:05:36.790 --> 01:05:38.720
I was a pariah.

01:05:38.720 --> 01:05:41.407
And so I didn't have the places, I didn't have the things,

01:05:41.407 --> 01:05:42.690
and I didn't have the people.

01:05:42.690 --> 01:05:45.140
So that hard bottom was a blessing.

01:05:45.140 --> 01:05:46.710
Yeah.

01:05:46.710 --> 01:05:49.770
And it had to, you know, force you to rightsize your ego

01:05:49.770 --> 01:05:52.610
and take stock and inventory of how you were living

01:05:52.610 --> 01:05:57.360
and figure out a new approach to how you were going to

01:05:57.360 --> 01:05:58.261
get things done.

01:05:58.261 --> 01:05:59.233
Yeah, inch by inch.

01:05:59.233 --> 01:06:03.280
Big thoughts like that probably didn't happen for awhile.

01:06:03.280 --> 01:06:04.730
I was just trying to survive.

01:06:07.570 --> 01:06:10.060
Many of our guests so far, including Jeff,

01:06:10.060 --> 01:06:12.780
have referenced the larger emotional context

01:06:12.780 --> 01:06:15.190
within which their addiction began.

01:06:15.190 --> 01:06:18.740
Psychological backstories that drove them to use

01:06:18.740 --> 01:06:22.960
and abuse substances, including the need for validation,

01:06:22.960 --> 01:06:26.800
things like bullying, physical or emotional abuse;

01:06:26.800 --> 01:06:29.040
in other words, trauma.

01:06:29.040 --> 01:06:31.260
And there is no better person to speak

01:06:31.260 --> 01:06:33.250
on the way trauma affects addiction,

01:06:33.250 --> 01:06:35.513
than my next guest, Dr. Gabor Mate.

01:06:36.420 --> 01:06:39.860
Dr. Mate is a world renowned bestselling author, lecturer,

01:06:39.860 --> 01:06:41.010
and a specialist

01:06:41.010 --> 01:06:43.463
on the cutting edge of addiction medicine research,

01:06:43.463 --> 01:06:46.700
with some fascinating and somewhat controversial,

01:06:46.700 --> 01:06:48.690
but I think quite regulatory ideas

01:06:48.690 --> 01:06:51.680
about the nexus between trauma,

01:06:51.680 --> 01:06:54.900
early childhood development and addiction.

01:06:54.900 --> 01:06:57.020
With over 20 years of medical practice

01:06:57.020 --> 01:06:59.010
and family and palliative care,

01:06:59.010 --> 01:07:02.040
Dr. Mate has decades of firsthand experience

01:07:02.040 --> 01:07:04.370
working with hardcore drug addicts.

01:07:04.370 --> 01:07:06.600
I find his insights quite compelling,

01:07:06.600 --> 01:07:09.920
specifically this idea that the source of addiction

01:07:09.920 --> 01:07:11.650
is not to be found in the genes,

01:07:11.650 --> 01:07:16.350
but in fact, originates in the early childhood environment.

01:07:16.350 --> 01:07:18.370
The idea that addiction is complex

01:07:18.370 --> 01:07:19.950
and that there is no quick fix.

01:07:19.950 --> 01:07:23.960
And that we should be advocating for a more compassionate,

01:07:23.960 --> 01:07:28.550
less punitive approach to addicts, addiction and treatment.

01:07:28.550 --> 01:07:30.490
I think you're gonna really enjoy this perspective.

01:07:30.490 --> 01:07:33.330
So without further ado, this is me

01:07:33.330 --> 01:07:34.680
and Dr. Gabor Mate.

01:07:36.110 --> 01:07:38.820
What's wrong with our kind of conventional wisdom

01:07:38.820 --> 01:07:40.340
around the idea of addiction

01:07:40.340 --> 01:07:43.530
and where your kind of philosophy and ideas coming.

01:07:43.530 --> 01:07:45.794
Well, traditional wisdom around addiction

01:07:45.794 --> 01:07:50.300
is either the legalistic one that has it

01:07:50.300 --> 01:07:53.380
as a bad choice that people make,

01:07:53.380 --> 01:07:55.020
from which they have to be deterred

01:07:55.020 --> 01:07:57.970
by means of severe punishment

01:07:57.970 --> 01:08:01.230
tends to be what the so-called criminal justice system,

01:08:01.230 --> 01:08:02.560
which by the way, I think it's a good title.

01:08:02.560 --> 01:08:04.250
It is a criminal system.

01:08:04.250 --> 01:08:08.520
The justice system is criminal, the way it treats people.

01:08:08.520 --> 01:08:09.860
Well, specifically addicts,

01:08:09.860 --> 01:08:11.780
sort of further traumatizing

01:08:11.780 --> 01:08:13.643
the people that are most traumatized.

01:08:13.643 --> 01:08:14.476
That's my point.

01:08:14.476 --> 01:08:16.950
Now the point though, is that that's the legalistic view

01:08:16.950 --> 01:08:20.060
that addiction is a choice and people need to be punished

01:08:20.060 --> 01:08:24.450
when they engage in addiction-related behaviors,

01:08:24.450 --> 01:08:27.210
their very addiction being illegal.

01:08:27.210 --> 01:08:30.200
Now the other perspective that seems different

01:08:30.200 --> 01:08:32.450
is that addiction is a brain disease

01:08:32.450 --> 01:08:33.790
that you largely inherit.

01:08:33.790 --> 01:08:36.299
So it's not your fault 'cause you can't help

01:08:36.299 --> 01:08:37.650
what genes you inherited,

01:08:37.650 --> 01:08:40.750
but it's a disease of the brain that arises in the brain;

01:08:40.750 --> 01:08:43.363
partly for genetic, partly for other reasons.

01:08:45.858 --> 01:08:49.500
And what both of those perspectives share though,

01:08:49.500 --> 01:08:50.570
different as they are,

01:08:50.570 --> 01:08:53.330
what they share is that in neither case

01:08:53.330 --> 01:08:55.402
that we're looking at people's life histories,

01:08:55.402 --> 01:08:57.350
and we're not looking at a social history

01:08:57.350 --> 01:09:00.540
or the history of a country or a nation.

01:09:00.540 --> 01:09:04.040
So who are the populations that are more severely affected

01:09:04.040 --> 01:09:05.320
and what happened to them?

01:09:05.320 --> 01:09:07.740
And who are the individuals that are most severely effected

01:09:07.740 --> 01:09:09.240
and what happens to them?

01:09:09.240 --> 01:09:10.743
Now, my perspective is that,

01:09:12.644 --> 01:09:16.450
addictions are attempts to soothe pain in every case.

01:09:16.450 --> 01:09:18.960
In fact, the drugs are specifically painkillers,

01:09:18.960 --> 01:09:21.175
cocaine numbs nerve endings,

01:09:21.175 --> 01:09:23.970
opiates, heroin, these are painkillers,

01:09:23.970 --> 01:09:26.470
alcohol is a painkiller, cannabis is a painkiller.

01:09:27.600 --> 01:09:29.970
Crystal meth diverts you from the experience

01:09:29.970 --> 01:09:32.280
of emotional suffering by making you feel more alive

01:09:32.280 --> 01:09:33.850
and excited temporarily.

01:09:33.850 --> 01:09:35.710
So it's always not why the addiction, as you said,

01:09:35.710 --> 01:09:37.110
but why the pain.

01:09:37.110 --> 01:09:39.800
Keith Richards, when he was talking

01:09:39.800 --> 01:09:42.390
about his heroin habit in his book, "Life,"

01:09:42.390 --> 01:09:45.699
which is autobiography, he said,

01:09:45.699 --> 01:09:47.430
and I'm almost quoting on verbatim.

01:09:47.430 --> 01:09:49.820
He said, "The contortions we go through

01:09:49.820 --> 01:09:52.560
just not to be ourselves for a few hours."

01:09:52.560 --> 01:09:54.900
Now, why would people not wanna be themselves?

01:09:54.900 --> 01:09:57.430
Because they're not comfortable in their own skins.

01:09:57.430 --> 01:09:59.770
Why are they not comfortable in their own skins?

01:09:59.770 --> 01:10:01.840
'Cause they suffered in their own skins

01:10:01.840 --> 01:10:04.530
at some point when they couldn't help it.

01:10:04.530 --> 01:10:07.440
So what I'm saying is that addiction, in every case,

01:10:07.440 --> 01:10:10.490
whether it's the severe addiction of the heroin addicts

01:10:10.490 --> 01:10:12.223
that I dealt with,

01:10:12.223 --> 01:10:16.653
or the respectable addictions of the workaholic,

01:10:17.780 --> 01:10:18.613
or,

01:10:20.910 --> 01:10:22.900
I'm probably free to mention that you talked

01:10:22.900 --> 01:10:26.980
about your own addiction at some point to extreme

01:10:26.980 --> 01:10:28.299
and you're in sports.

01:10:28.299 --> 01:10:31.560
And these are always based in trauma.

01:10:31.560 --> 01:10:33.807
So any attempt to escape the present moment

01:10:33.807 --> 01:10:38.540
has to do with discomfort that we incurred as children.

01:10:38.540 --> 01:10:41.430
And there are degrees of discomfort, degrees of trauma.

01:10:41.430 --> 01:10:43.720
But fundamentally addiction is always an attempt

01:10:43.720 --> 01:10:45.550
to escape suffering.

01:10:45.550 --> 01:10:47.240
So it's not the problem.

01:10:47.240 --> 01:10:48.890
The addiction is not the problem.

01:10:50.026 --> 01:10:53.028
The manifestation of the addiction

01:10:53.028 --> 01:10:53.900
is the solution to the problem.

01:10:53.900 --> 01:10:55.635
It's an attempt and...

01:10:55.635 --> 01:10:56.650
And it works until it doesn't work anymore.

01:10:56.650 --> 01:10:58.110
It works temporarily.

01:10:58.110 --> 01:10:59.673
In fact, that's my definition of addiction.

01:10:59.673 --> 01:11:01.973
It's a temporary relief pleasure,

01:11:03.921 --> 01:11:08.190
craving that is satisfied momentarily,

01:11:08.190 --> 01:11:10.290
but creates negative consequences in the long-term,

01:11:10.290 --> 01:11:11.857
and you can't give it up.

01:11:11.857 --> 01:11:12.808
That's what an addiction is.

01:11:12.808 --> 01:11:13.641
But yeah, that's what it is.

01:11:13.641 --> 01:11:16.850
It's an attempted solution, it's not the primary problem.

01:11:16.850 --> 01:11:19.570
And so to say that addiction is a primary brain disease,

01:11:19.570 --> 01:11:21.846
which is the official medical perspective

01:11:21.846 --> 01:11:24.960
that I was trained in misses the whole point.

01:11:24.960 --> 01:11:29.910
But then again, the medical profession notoriously

01:11:29.910 --> 01:11:32.010
does not understand trauma.

01:11:32.010 --> 01:11:33.560
The research is totally clear.

01:11:33.560 --> 01:11:36.830
Like, it's not that I'm giving you personal insights.

01:11:36.830 --> 01:11:38.160
I mean, I am,

01:11:38.160 --> 01:11:40.670
but these insights are very much also supported

01:11:40.670 --> 01:11:44.700
and grounded in a vast body of literature.

01:11:44.700 --> 01:11:48.200
And whether we're talking about addiction or cancer,

01:11:48.200 --> 01:11:50.830
you can look back to negative childhood experiences.

01:11:50.830 --> 01:11:55.830
So even on the level of physiological changes in the brain,

01:11:56.160 --> 01:11:57.420
we're looking at the same changes;

01:11:57.420 --> 01:12:00.100
just as some people use and need chemicals

01:12:00.100 --> 01:12:01.430
to achieve those changes,

01:12:01.430 --> 01:12:03.030
other people get it through behaviors.

01:12:03.030 --> 01:12:06.340
But the gambler is still after a dopamine hit,

01:12:06.340 --> 01:12:09.060
dopamine being the incentive motivation chemical.

01:12:09.060 --> 01:12:11.480
He's not after the money,

01:12:11.480 --> 01:12:12.470
'cause it was about the money,

01:12:12.470 --> 01:12:15.560
he would quit after he won his first jackpot.

01:12:15.560 --> 01:12:18.910
But it's about actually the dopamine hit that he gets,

01:12:18.910 --> 01:12:21.610
that temporary state of elation and excitement

01:12:21.610 --> 01:12:23.830
that he gets when he's engaged in the activity.

01:12:23.830 --> 01:12:28.830
So, all addictions, whether they're behaviors

01:12:28.940 --> 01:12:30.650
or substances or whatever they are,

01:12:30.650 --> 01:12:34.014
they serve the same psychological purpose

01:12:34.014 --> 01:12:38.500
of escape from suffering and pain, discomfort with the self,

01:12:38.500 --> 01:12:41.290
and they activate the same brain circuits

01:12:41.290 --> 01:12:43.380
with the same neuro-chemicals.

01:12:43.380 --> 01:12:45.762
So there's only one universal addiction process.

01:12:45.762 --> 01:12:48.803
Drug addiction being a small,

01:12:48.803 --> 01:12:51.460
small manifestation of that.

01:12:51.460 --> 01:12:52.815
Right.

01:12:52.815 --> 01:12:54.500
And because it's so divorced from logic

01:12:54.500 --> 01:12:55.840
and rational thinking,

01:12:55.840 --> 01:12:58.340
I think that's what prevents a lot of people

01:12:58.340 --> 01:13:00.070
from really, truly understanding it.

01:13:00.070 --> 01:13:02.500
And why the conversation around it

01:13:02.500 --> 01:13:06.947
has to do with judgment and shame and criminology.

01:13:06.947 --> 01:13:10.000
Well, I think if you wanna explore

01:13:10.000 --> 01:13:12.329
that mine field, we can.

01:13:12.329 --> 01:13:15.270
I think on the individual level,

01:13:15.270 --> 01:13:18.680
the judgment and this thing for drug addicts

01:13:18.680 --> 01:13:23.680
is actually well expressed by Jesus when He says,

01:13:24.500 --> 01:13:26.340
don't be hypocrite, He says.

01:13:26.340 --> 01:13:27.490
That's the word He uses.

01:13:27.490 --> 01:13:30.850
He says, before you try to remove the sliver

01:13:30.850 --> 01:13:34.090
from your brothers eye, remove the poll from your own eye.

01:13:34.090 --> 01:13:36.170
And so what it is

01:13:36.170 --> 01:13:37.980
is that when there's something we don't like

01:13:37.980 --> 01:13:42.980
about ourselves, then we'll look at that same thing

01:13:43.470 --> 01:13:45.400
as somebody else and reject it.

01:13:45.400 --> 01:13:47.620
And imagine that we're different.

01:13:47.620 --> 01:13:50.330
So the workaholic businessman, or the workaholic doctor,

01:13:50.330 --> 01:13:51.173
for that matter,

01:13:52.100 --> 01:13:56.900
or the shopaholic man or woman.

01:13:56.900 --> 01:13:58.920
The guy who spends $8,000 on CDs.

01:13:58.920 --> 01:14:01.950
CDs in a week, I've heard of people like that.

01:14:01.950 --> 01:14:02.783
And,

01:14:04.630 --> 01:14:07.660
they like to think of themselves as superior

01:14:07.660 --> 01:14:09.570
to the drug addict.

01:14:09.570 --> 01:14:11.270
And yet the dynamic is the same;

01:14:11.270 --> 01:14:15.260
which is being in the grip of a compulsion

01:14:15.260 --> 01:14:16.640
that you can't control.

01:14:16.640 --> 01:14:18.390
And that has negative consequences.

01:14:18.390 --> 01:14:19.770
And you don't have the power.

01:14:19.770 --> 01:14:22.008
At least you perceive that you don't have the power

01:14:22.008 --> 01:14:24.920
to regulate yourself.

01:14:24.920 --> 01:14:27.260
And so we see that in somebody else.

01:14:27.260 --> 01:14:31.140
And it's so easy then to look at the externals,

01:14:31.140 --> 01:14:32.930
which is as somebody who's in a back alley

01:14:32.930 --> 01:14:34.510
in the downtown east side of Vancouver,

01:14:34.510 --> 01:14:36.720
shooting over with heroin or cocaine,

01:14:36.720 --> 01:14:38.390
or they're different from the rest of us.

01:14:38.390 --> 01:14:39.280
No, they're not.

01:14:39.280 --> 01:14:41.690
They're only different in their expression

01:14:41.690 --> 01:14:42.523
of their addiction.

01:14:42.523 --> 01:14:43.640
And they're also different

01:14:43.640 --> 01:14:48.490
in their social-economic background often.

01:14:48.490 --> 01:14:50.650
And also of course, in the degree of trauma

01:14:50.650 --> 01:14:52.754
that they experienced as children.

01:14:52.754 --> 01:14:55.260
So when we talk about addiction as a brain disease,

01:14:55.260 --> 01:14:56.810
yes, there's truth to that.

01:14:56.810 --> 01:14:59.120
But what shapes the brain is the environment.

01:14:59.120 --> 01:15:02.400
And the necessary condition for healthy brain development

01:15:02.400 --> 01:15:04.160
is non-stressed parents

01:15:04.160 --> 01:15:06.100
who can really connect with the child.

01:15:06.100 --> 01:15:07.691
So you can see in our society,

01:15:07.691 --> 01:15:09.820
why so many people are affected,

01:15:09.820 --> 01:15:12.060
'cause how many parents are non-stressed?

01:15:12.060 --> 01:15:14.042
How many parents have the kind of support

01:15:14.042 --> 01:15:16.830
that traditionalist societies used to provide?

01:15:16.830 --> 01:15:19.247
The clan, the tribe, the extended family

01:15:19.247 --> 01:15:21.463
where the child is always around adults

01:15:21.463 --> 01:15:23.280
who are looking after 'em.

01:15:23.280 --> 01:15:26.130
Not that I want to romanticize the past

01:15:26.130 --> 01:15:29.830
or nor that we can go back to it, but we've lost something.

01:15:29.830 --> 01:15:31.128
And so in this society,

01:15:31.128 --> 01:15:32.790
it was just so disconnected, alienated.

01:15:32.790 --> 01:15:34.620
Parents are, even if they're together,

01:15:34.620 --> 01:15:36.670
and 40% of the time, they're not.

01:15:36.670 --> 01:15:39.310
But even if they are, they're both having to go to work,

01:15:39.310 --> 01:15:42.670
they're both under severe strain, economically very often,

01:15:42.670 --> 01:15:44.483
relationship stresses,

01:15:45.550 --> 01:15:47.880
the spiritual emptiness in people's lives.

01:15:47.880 --> 01:15:50.810
Kids are being born into situations

01:15:50.810 --> 01:15:52.867
that no longer support healthy brain development.

01:15:52.867 --> 01:15:55.620
And on top of that, they're actually abused,

01:15:55.620 --> 01:15:59.360
which is what happens to most severe addicts,

01:15:59.360 --> 01:16:02.670
specific abuse in a certain form of physical beating,

01:16:02.670 --> 01:16:06.570
sexual exploitation, abandonment, neglect,

01:16:06.570 --> 01:16:09.860
emotional torment, that plays havoc,

01:16:09.860 --> 01:16:12.300
not only with the personality development,

01:16:12.300 --> 01:16:13.990
not only does it give you a lot of pain

01:16:13.990 --> 01:16:16.471
that later you have to just suit somehow,

01:16:16.471 --> 01:16:19.770
but it also distorts your brain development.

01:16:19.770 --> 01:16:22.220
And then suddenly you have the template for addiction.

01:16:22.220 --> 01:16:25.540
A child under severe emotional stress

01:16:26.862 --> 01:16:30.326
has got real limited resources to deal with that.

01:16:30.326 --> 01:16:33.490
They can't fight back, they can't escape

01:16:33.490 --> 01:16:35.413
and they can't change the situation.

01:16:36.670 --> 01:16:40.210
So now the brain kicks in with its automatic defenses.

01:16:40.210 --> 01:16:42.760
One of them is emotional shutdown.

01:16:42.760 --> 01:16:44.580
So now you no longer feel the emotion

01:16:44.580 --> 01:16:45.830
'cause they're too painful.

01:16:45.830 --> 01:16:47.300
But if you don't feel emotion,

01:16:47.300 --> 01:16:49.010
life becomes very dull and boring.

01:16:49.010 --> 01:16:51.163
You may have to do drugs to feel better.

01:16:54.460 --> 01:16:57.340
Or you might tune out as a way of

01:16:59.790 --> 01:17:02.280
not experiencing the stress.

01:17:02.280 --> 01:17:04.820
So you adapt by tuning out.

01:17:04.820 --> 01:17:06.960
But if that gets programmed into your brain,

01:17:06.960 --> 01:17:09.310
later on, you've got this condition called ADD.

01:17:10.290 --> 01:17:13.000
Which is characterized by extreme tuning out.

01:17:13.000 --> 01:17:16.290
So what happens is that these early defenses

01:17:16.290 --> 01:17:19.090
help kids survive the immediate stress,

01:17:19.090 --> 01:17:22.120
but become sources of dysfunction later on.

01:17:22.120 --> 01:17:24.490
So as a matter of fact,

01:17:24.490 --> 01:17:27.186
I mean, as you all know from recovery,

01:17:27.186 --> 01:17:29.867
what makes the difference ultimately

01:17:29.867 --> 01:17:34.867
is not just one's own individual willpower

01:17:34.945 --> 01:17:37.480
and determination,

01:17:37.480 --> 01:17:42.250
but also others who can listen to you compassionately

01:17:42.250 --> 01:17:44.600
and validate your experience and not judge you.

01:17:46.130 --> 01:17:48.150
We've heard many recovery accounts so far

01:17:48.150 --> 01:17:49.810
that sound very linear.

01:17:49.810 --> 01:17:51.230
They were in the throes of addiction,

01:17:51.230 --> 01:17:52.390
they hit a rock bottom,

01:17:52.390 --> 01:17:56.430
and then they find longterm recovery shortly thereafter.

01:17:56.430 --> 01:17:59.060
But what about the people who try recovery many times

01:17:59.060 --> 01:18:01.210
before it finally sticks?

01:18:01.210 --> 01:18:03.580
What is it about the final attempt

01:18:03.580 --> 01:18:06.250
that makes their recovery last?

01:18:06.250 --> 01:18:08.170
And what should an addict do

01:18:08.170 --> 01:18:13.170
when they recoil against the idea of a "higher power?"

01:18:13.330 --> 01:18:15.650
Well, this is the focus of this next clip,

01:18:15.650 --> 01:18:17.450
featuring Charlie Engle.

01:18:17.450 --> 01:18:19.300
Charlie is a husband, father,

01:18:19.300 --> 01:18:23.410
and celebrated ultra marathoner who has run across deserts,

01:18:23.410 --> 01:18:25.830
and summited ice covered volcanoes,

01:18:25.830 --> 01:18:27.890
has swam with crocodiles,

01:18:27.890 --> 01:18:30.870
and even served a stint in federal prison.

01:18:30.870 --> 01:18:35.120
But at his core, he is a sober addict.

01:18:35.120 --> 01:18:36.760
Charlie told his whole story

01:18:36.760 --> 01:18:40.390
way back in the early days of the podcast, episode 67,

01:18:40.390 --> 01:18:42.210
which is absolutely epic.

01:18:42.210 --> 01:18:43.610
You got to check it out.

01:18:43.610 --> 01:18:47.810
And this clip is from his second appearance, episode, 248.

01:18:47.810 --> 01:18:49.120
In addition to talking about

01:18:49.120 --> 01:18:52.540
why his recovery didn't stick the first time,

01:18:52.540 --> 01:18:56.100
we discuss a common question we both get regarding,

01:18:56.100 --> 01:18:59.980
if we have, in fact, just transferred our prior addictions

01:18:59.980 --> 01:19:02.260
onto our current running.

01:19:02.260 --> 01:19:05.560
As well as the perfect summary of what an alcoholic is

01:19:05.560 --> 01:19:06.830
at their core.

01:19:06.830 --> 01:19:09.963
So for that and more, here is Charlie Engle.

01:19:10.860 --> 01:19:11.990
Man, I'll just tell you very briefly,

01:19:11.990 --> 01:19:15.130
the reason I know this for certain,

01:19:15.130 --> 01:19:17.460
the reason treatment didn't work for me when I went,

01:19:17.460 --> 01:19:18.513
when I was 27,

01:19:19.760 --> 01:19:23.980
was, you know, 28 day program in Pacific Grove, California,

01:19:23.980 --> 01:19:26.540
traditional, you know, you do the group counseling,

01:19:26.540 --> 01:19:28.930
get some one-on-one, you go to AA meetings

01:19:28.930 --> 01:19:31.812
that are right there in the building and all that,

01:19:31.812 --> 01:19:35.830
is because I was a genius, right?

01:19:35.830 --> 01:19:37.280
So, I mean, that was my view.

01:19:38.239 --> 01:19:39.183
And I didn't really think I was a genius,

01:19:40.380 --> 01:19:41.279
but I was smart.

01:19:41.279 --> 01:19:43.333
And smart people don't need spirituality.

01:19:43.333 --> 01:19:44.850
That was my...

01:19:44.850 --> 01:19:46.880
They also don't need to do the work

01:19:46.880 --> 01:19:49.960
because they can read the book or skim it and go, I got it.

01:19:49.960 --> 01:19:51.415
I got this figured out.

01:19:51.415 --> 01:19:52.314
Absolutely.

01:19:52.314 --> 01:19:54.210
So I said, almost like training for a marathon,

01:19:54.210 --> 01:19:56.640
I said, okay, I'm gonna do this for 90 days.

01:19:56.640 --> 01:19:59.638
So I practically learned that bedbug word for word,

01:19:59.638 --> 01:20:03.270
I could, you know, I'd say the serenity prayer,

01:20:03.270 --> 01:20:05.880
I would certainly talk about a higher power.

01:20:05.880 --> 01:20:08.020
But in my head I might, yeah, whatever,

01:20:08.020 --> 01:20:10.050
that's for other people.

01:20:10.050 --> 01:20:11.490
I'll do the rest of this.

01:20:11.490 --> 01:20:13.130
And I did get a lot out of,

01:20:13.130 --> 01:20:16.210
you know, doing an inventory and sharing it with somebody

01:20:16.210 --> 01:20:18.754
and talking about this.

01:20:18.754 --> 01:20:22.390
I understand very quickly that doing all of that

01:20:22.390 --> 01:20:26.380
was a way of purging and therapy and it did feel good.

01:20:26.380 --> 01:20:31.380
But I wasn't about to allow some kind of higher power to...

01:20:31.530 --> 01:20:33.580
I wouldn't turn a shit over to that guy.

01:20:33.580 --> 01:20:34.793
You know what I mean?

01:20:34.793 --> 01:20:36.640
'Cause that would require you to relinquish,

01:20:36.640 --> 01:20:38.550
sell yourself wealth, right?

01:20:38.550 --> 01:20:42.430
And as somebody, you know, beyond the intelligence quotient,

01:20:42.430 --> 01:20:45.360
you're also an athlete and an athlete who excels

01:20:45.360 --> 01:20:47.330
in an individual sport.

01:20:47.330 --> 01:20:48.790
And if there's something you learned

01:20:48.790 --> 01:20:51.300
through the process of developing as an athlete

01:20:51.300 --> 01:20:53.210
in an individual sport,

01:20:53.210 --> 01:20:55.890
is that your performance is directly related

01:20:55.890 --> 01:20:58.600
to the amount of work and focus and dedication

01:20:58.600 --> 01:21:01.130
that you and you alone decide to put into it.

01:21:01.130 --> 01:21:03.700
And what comes out of that is this idea

01:21:03.700 --> 01:21:07.830
that self-will will avail you everything, right?

01:21:07.830 --> 01:21:11.110
In the context of running or any other number of sports.

01:21:11.110 --> 01:21:14.370
So to then say, well, you got to let go of that.

01:21:14.370 --> 01:21:18.010
Like, not only does that create something to disagree with,

01:21:18.010 --> 01:21:19.040
it doesn't even compute.

01:21:19.040 --> 01:21:20.290
Like, what do you mean?

01:21:20.290 --> 01:21:21.646
Right?

01:21:21.646 --> 01:21:22.860
Like the idea of surrendering that,

01:21:22.860 --> 01:21:24.260
I don't even understand with that.

01:21:24.260 --> 01:21:26.769
No, so 90 days into treatment,

01:21:26.769 --> 01:21:28.154
I mean, it's 28 day treatment.

01:21:28.154 --> 01:21:29.940
And then I stayed sober for 60 more days

01:21:29.940 --> 01:21:32.180
where I was going to meetings and I was active.

01:21:32.180 --> 01:21:34.620
And man, I was waiting for that 90 days tip

01:21:34.620 --> 01:21:38.420
because that was like a graduation diploma

01:21:38.420 --> 01:21:39.673
for me in my mind.

01:21:39.673 --> 01:21:41.010
That I knew that.

01:21:41.010 --> 01:21:43.530
Like, I wasn't telling anybody else that,

01:21:43.530 --> 01:21:45.430
but if I could just get to 90 days,

01:21:45.430 --> 01:21:49.080
then things would be fine from that point forward.

01:21:49.080 --> 01:21:51.360
And so I stopped going to meetings.

01:21:51.360 --> 01:21:53.750
I certainly didn't have a sponsor to talk to.

01:21:53.750 --> 01:21:58.479
And I did manage to stay sober another like 90 days.

01:21:58.479 --> 01:22:01.680
But what I didn't get at that point

01:22:01.680 --> 01:22:04.840
was that there's only about what?

01:22:04.840 --> 01:22:09.050
There's only like 1% of the time for a recovering addict

01:22:09.050 --> 01:22:11.110
that is actually dangerous.

01:22:11.110 --> 01:22:13.910
And the other 99% of the time

01:22:13.910 --> 01:22:17.440
is when we're supposed to be building up our power

01:22:17.440 --> 01:22:21.580
and our guards to be prepared for that 1%.

01:22:21.580 --> 01:22:24.900
That one moment when a drink appears or a drug appears

01:22:24.900 --> 01:22:29.030
or a feeling, heaven forbid, appears.

01:22:29.030 --> 01:22:32.640
An motion that we're not prepared for that in the past,

01:22:32.640 --> 01:22:34.690
we could take care of that emotion in a second

01:22:34.690 --> 01:22:36.920
with a drug or a beer or whatever.

01:22:36.920 --> 01:22:41.750
And so, I didn't understand that all of this time

01:22:41.750 --> 01:22:43.530
was like training for a race.

01:22:43.530 --> 01:22:47.640
You're training 99% of the time, and the race has only 1%.

01:22:47.640 --> 01:22:49.860
That race is that one moment

01:22:49.860 --> 01:22:54.100
where suddenly you're like, what's the big deal?

01:22:54.100 --> 01:22:54.933
I can have a beer.

01:22:54.933 --> 01:22:57.345
You got this thing in the back of your head,

01:22:57.345 --> 01:22:59.890
this voice going, hey, you know, you deserve this.

01:22:59.890 --> 01:23:02.330
You don't just need a beer, you deserve it.

01:23:02.330 --> 01:23:04.308
Damn it, you've worked hard.

01:23:04.308 --> 01:23:08.130
So at nine, it was at around nine months, right?

01:23:08.130 --> 01:23:10.800
So at nine months, you take your diploma,

01:23:10.800 --> 01:23:12.560
you graduate from sobriety

01:23:12.560 --> 01:23:15.166
and celebrate this great achievement

01:23:15.166 --> 01:23:18.410
by going out on a bender, right?

01:23:18.410 --> 01:23:20.870
And so when people come to me and they're like,

01:23:20.870 --> 01:23:22.870
you know, like I'm not an alcoholic.

01:23:22.870 --> 01:23:25.980
I'm not sure I really understand what that means

01:23:25.980 --> 01:23:27.930
or what that is, like, what is an alcoholic?

01:23:27.930 --> 01:23:28.770
What is an addict?

01:23:28.770 --> 01:23:30.433
And I just, I always say,

01:23:31.930 --> 01:23:35.090
imagine a person who works extremely hard

01:23:35.090 --> 01:23:36.540
to repair their life.

01:23:36.540 --> 01:23:38.670
They stay sober for an entire year.

01:23:38.670 --> 01:23:40.550
They put everything into it.

01:23:40.550 --> 01:23:42.910
They make it their number one priority.

01:23:42.910 --> 01:23:45.095
And then when they get that one year chip,

01:23:45.095 --> 01:23:47.420
they celebrate it by getting drunk

01:23:47.420 --> 01:23:49.710
and they think that's a good idea.

01:23:49.710 --> 01:23:52.530
That is the essence of what it means to be an alcoholic.

01:23:52.530 --> 01:23:55.990
The insanity of that tweak, that mental tweak,

01:23:55.990 --> 01:23:59.540
that mental, you know, whatever it is,

01:23:59.540 --> 01:24:03.000
disrepair that would lead you to believe

01:24:03.000 --> 01:24:04.700
that that's a perfectly good idea.

01:24:04.700 --> 01:24:06.123
Well, and here's the thing.

01:24:07.089 --> 01:24:10.590
The discipline that it took to go that year and to know,

01:24:10.590 --> 01:24:13.717
and probably in the back of your head that,

01:24:13.717 --> 01:24:16.420
I mean, that year didn't just appear

01:24:16.420 --> 01:24:17.860
and then you decided to get drunk.

01:24:17.860 --> 01:24:20.916
Like that thought of drinking started.

01:24:20.916 --> 01:24:22.197
Oh yeah, you're planting that seed.

01:24:22.197 --> 01:24:24.320
And so, how much discipline does it take

01:24:24.320 --> 01:24:26.190
to actually keep yourself in that space

01:24:26.190 --> 01:24:28.200
once you already know this is gonna happen?

01:24:28.200 --> 01:24:31.108
I mean, so the power of an alcoholic or an addict,

01:24:31.108 --> 01:24:34.520
it's so strong.

01:24:34.520 --> 01:24:37.478
And if that could only be used,

01:24:37.478 --> 01:24:40.100
if our power can be used for good,

01:24:40.100 --> 01:24:42.570
then a lot can be accomplished.

01:24:42.570 --> 01:24:44.940
And that is why though,

01:24:44.940 --> 01:24:47.730
I think, you know, you and I and a lot of other people

01:24:47.730 --> 01:24:50.800
that you talk to and that are listening out there right now,

01:24:50.800 --> 01:24:53.860
they do understand that,

01:24:53.860 --> 01:24:57.960
man, this addictive power is what makes them good at stuff.

01:24:57.960 --> 01:25:00.554
And if they can just figure out

01:25:00.554 --> 01:25:02.950
how to apply it to their life.

01:25:02.950 --> 01:25:03.810
Right.

01:25:03.810 --> 01:25:04.850
But then when you do that,

01:25:04.850 --> 01:25:06.730
they accuse you of just being addictive

01:25:06.730 --> 01:25:08.170
in that other discipline.

01:25:08.170 --> 01:25:09.780
Which brings us up, and we talked about this last time,

01:25:09.780 --> 01:25:11.099
but I think it bears repeating,

01:25:11.099 --> 01:25:14.663
or maybe your definition or perception about has evolved.

01:25:14.663 --> 01:25:17.190
When people say, well, you're still,

01:25:17.190 --> 01:25:18.460
you're just a crazy alcoholic.

01:25:18.460 --> 01:25:20.600
You just channeled all that into your running

01:25:20.600 --> 01:25:23.020
and you have this unhealthy relationship with it.

01:25:23.020 --> 01:25:24.060
And it couldn't be.

01:25:24.060 --> 01:25:26.490
It literally couldn't be farther from the truth,

01:25:26.490 --> 01:25:29.690
because everything about my addict

01:25:29.690 --> 01:25:31.860
and my addictive behavior when I was drinking

01:25:31.860 --> 01:25:36.860
and doing drugs was about hiding every bit of who I was.

01:25:37.180 --> 01:25:39.100
I mean, first of all, I didn't really know who I was,

01:25:39.100 --> 01:25:41.510
but heaven forbid that starts to surface,

01:25:41.510 --> 01:25:44.930
I would drink it or drug it down back into the pit

01:25:44.930 --> 01:25:45.920
where it came from.

01:25:45.920 --> 01:25:50.500
So all I wanted to do was destroy and hide

01:25:50.500 --> 01:25:53.150
every bit of my personality.

01:25:53.150 --> 01:25:57.710
Running on the other hand does the exact opposite.

01:25:57.710 --> 01:26:02.710
I really do feel like I become the true essence

01:26:03.680 --> 01:26:08.450
of who I am and who I'm meant to be in the course of a run.

01:26:08.450 --> 01:26:11.380
And that doesn't mean it's all light and sunshine.

01:26:11.380 --> 01:26:13.500
Sometimes that means I'm a dick.

01:26:13.500 --> 01:26:15.850
Sometimes that means I'm whatever.

01:26:15.850 --> 01:26:20.570
But it's an emotion that I actually get to have.

01:26:20.570 --> 01:26:21.990
So maybe my view has evolved.

01:26:21.990 --> 01:26:23.550
I think this is different than what I said,

01:26:23.550 --> 01:26:25.277
but it's more expansive.

01:26:25.277 --> 01:26:27.490
I know understand that I'm not,

01:26:27.490 --> 01:26:31.910
it's not a requirement just because I'm sober

01:26:31.910 --> 01:26:33.750
that life is going to be good,

01:26:33.750 --> 01:26:36.070
or that I have to be nice all the time,

01:26:36.070 --> 01:26:39.190
or that I'm not gonna be hungry or cranky

01:26:39.190 --> 01:26:43.890
or snap at my wife on something when I absolutely shouldn't.

01:26:43.890 --> 01:26:48.210
The difference is now I get to apologize.

01:26:48.210 --> 01:26:51.500
I get to admit my wrong,

01:26:51.500 --> 01:26:55.530
or even potentially stick by my rightness

01:26:55.530 --> 01:27:00.530
but without that alcoholics conviction of based in fear

01:27:01.350 --> 01:27:05.634
that everything I do has to be justified in some way.

01:27:05.634 --> 01:27:10.401
And running has taught me that I...

01:27:10.401 --> 01:27:11.234
And it doesn't have to be running.

01:27:11.234 --> 01:27:12.667
It can be biking.

01:27:12.667 --> 01:27:13.800
I mean, if I couldn't run again tomorrow,

01:27:13.800 --> 01:27:16.130
I'd find another outlet for this.

01:27:16.130 --> 01:27:19.710
And I don't do it additively, I do it instinctively.

01:27:19.710 --> 01:27:20.900
I don't run every day.

01:27:20.900 --> 01:27:24.410
I like to exercise every day because it makes me feel good.

01:27:24.410 --> 01:27:26.650
Why the hell wouldn't I want to do that?

01:27:26.650 --> 01:27:30.563
Running saved my life, but then it actually gave me a life.

01:27:32.380 --> 01:27:34.320
Our next guest is Mishka Shubaly.

01:27:34.320 --> 01:27:35.570
Michigan is a dear friend

01:27:35.570 --> 01:27:38.430
and has been on the podcast many, many times.

01:27:38.430 --> 01:27:41.770
He's a writer and musician who pens true stories

01:27:41.770 --> 01:27:45.400
about drink, drugs, disasters, desire,

01:27:45.400 --> 01:27:47.490
deception and their aftermath.

01:27:47.490 --> 01:27:50.310
He is also the author of, "I Swear I'll Make It Up To You,"

01:27:50.310 --> 01:27:54.750
which is this brutally honest, booze fueled opiated account

01:27:54.750 --> 01:27:59.240
of addiction, abandonment, artistic frustration,

01:27:59.240 --> 01:28:02.880
faith, guilt, sobriety, running, resentment, music,

01:28:02.880 --> 01:28:05.700
and ultimately salvation.

01:28:05.700 --> 01:28:08.290
But in this final clip, Mishka discusses

01:28:08.290 --> 01:28:10.980
how to handle cravings when they appear,

01:28:10.980 --> 01:28:13.770
what to do if you can't fully separate yourself

01:28:13.770 --> 01:28:15.700
from a triggering environment.

01:28:15.700 --> 01:28:18.590
In Mishka's case, he still play shows in bars.

01:28:18.590 --> 01:28:20.810
And we end the clip with Mishka sharing about

01:28:20.810 --> 01:28:22.130
the power of community,

01:28:22.130 --> 01:28:26.760
and specifically, how our friendship helps keep him sober.

01:28:26.760 --> 01:28:28.470
I'm honored to have this man in my life,

01:28:28.470 --> 01:28:30.930
and I'm excited to share his perspective with you.

01:28:30.930 --> 01:28:32.150
So, here we go.

01:28:32.150 --> 01:28:34.273
This is me and Mishka Shubaly.

01:28:35.750 --> 01:28:38.900
This is kind of an endemic thing with alcoholics.

01:28:38.900 --> 01:28:41.470
Like, even people who have been sober for a long time,

01:28:41.470 --> 01:28:43.435
suddenly they have an urge

01:28:43.435 --> 01:28:44.360
and they feel bad about themselves.

01:28:44.360 --> 01:28:46.430
Like, I can't believe that I have that urge

01:28:46.430 --> 01:28:47.400
or that compulsion.

01:28:47.400 --> 01:28:48.810
Like, haven't I moved past this?

01:28:48.810 --> 01:28:52.160
But you have to remember that if you're an alcoholic,

01:28:52.160 --> 01:28:55.070
like you're a true, real alcoholic,

01:28:55.070 --> 01:28:58.700
that is your default state of mind and your condition.

01:28:58.700 --> 01:29:01.100
So you should just understand that

01:29:01.100 --> 01:29:02.870
that goes with the territory,

01:29:02.870 --> 01:29:04.780
and it's a little kind of like red flag,

01:29:04.780 --> 01:29:07.090
like, oh, maybe I need to take a little contrary action

01:29:07.090 --> 01:29:08.640
to get back on track.

01:29:08.640 --> 01:29:10.370
But of course, you're gonna feel that way

01:29:10.370 --> 01:29:11.970
from time to time.

01:29:11.970 --> 01:29:14.250
The miracle is that you don't act on that.

01:29:14.250 --> 01:29:15.890
It's like, you're gonna have those emotions.

01:29:15.890 --> 01:29:18.590
You're gonna feel that way periodically.

01:29:18.590 --> 01:29:20.410
It's what you do in response to that,

01:29:20.410 --> 01:29:22.840
that dictates life outcomes.

01:29:22.840 --> 01:29:27.270
And the miracle is that you're not drinking every day.

01:29:27.270 --> 01:29:29.060
Like you forget that, right?

01:29:29.060 --> 01:29:30.420
That's your default state.

01:29:30.420 --> 01:29:32.740
And you've put together eight years without doing that.

01:29:32.740 --> 01:29:34.370
So you have a craving from time to time.

01:29:34.370 --> 01:29:36.900
Congratulations, you're an alcoholic.

01:29:36.900 --> 01:29:37.810
You shouldn't be ashamed of that.

01:29:37.810 --> 01:29:40.000
It's just a little nudge to say,

01:29:40.000 --> 01:29:42.000
hey, maybe I should take a look

01:29:42.000 --> 01:29:44.757
at whatever is going on with me physically and emotionally

01:29:44.757 --> 01:29:46.390
that led to that craving,

01:29:46.390 --> 01:29:50.440
so I can maybe shift my behavior

01:29:50.440 --> 01:29:53.560
or my environment a little bit to nip that in the bud

01:29:53.560 --> 01:29:55.260
so that doesn't come up next time.

01:29:56.210 --> 01:29:58.003
It gets even more interesting.

01:29:58.920 --> 01:30:01.213
On this last tour in England,

01:30:03.058 --> 01:30:05.750
I was traveling with this band bird cloud

01:30:05.750 --> 01:30:08.411
who are alcohol aficionados.

01:30:08.411 --> 01:30:10.800
They appreciate the sauce.

01:30:10.800 --> 01:30:14.640
And they're brilliant musicians and brilliant writers.

01:30:14.640 --> 01:30:16.490
And it was a privilege to tour with them.

01:30:16.490 --> 01:30:20.420
And also day after day, I was like, ah.

01:30:20.420 --> 01:30:22.530
And then towards the end of the tour,

01:30:22.530 --> 01:30:24.910
we were in London and I had sort of like,

01:30:24.910 --> 01:30:27.403
you know, after you're on the road for a while,

01:30:27.403 --> 01:30:28.410
you get used to setting things up a certain way.

01:30:28.410 --> 01:30:30.580
I put my cable here, I put my slide here,

01:30:30.580 --> 01:30:33.770
I put my thing, water or seltzer here.

01:30:33.770 --> 01:30:36.970
And then I got up on stage.

01:30:36.970 --> 01:30:38.980
Great night, like sold out room.

01:30:38.980 --> 01:30:40.620
And to be in a foreign country

01:30:40.620 --> 01:30:42.290
and have people singing your songs back to you.

01:30:42.290 --> 01:30:45.410
I mean, that's like such a fun thing.

01:30:45.410 --> 01:30:49.143
And I went up and I like did my first song,

01:30:49.143 --> 01:30:51.620
reached down, grabbed my drink, took a swig of it,

01:30:51.620 --> 01:30:52.883
and it was pure vodka.

01:30:54.520 --> 01:30:56.440
It's not the first time this has happened to you.

01:30:56.440 --> 01:30:57.880
You brought about that other time.

01:30:57.880 --> 01:31:01.163
And that was a mixed vodka drink, this was straight vodka.

01:31:02.624 --> 01:31:04.970
And I was like, did somebody do this to me on purpose?

01:31:04.970 --> 01:31:06.080
Or like what the...

01:31:06.080 --> 01:31:09.761
But I felt it in my mouth and I spit it out

01:31:09.761 --> 01:31:12.480
back into the glass and I did the rest of the set

01:31:12.480 --> 01:31:16.240
totally cotton mouth, like not drinking anything.

01:31:16.240 --> 01:31:20.530
And that's weird to have something like that happen

01:31:20.530 --> 01:31:22.200
in the public eye,

01:31:22.200 --> 01:31:24.260
and then you have to go through the rest of the show.

01:31:24.260 --> 01:31:25.530
And the whole time I was thinking like,

01:31:25.530 --> 01:31:29.780
well, I already got a little bit, why not just, you know?

01:31:29.780 --> 01:31:31.660
Right, and you also have that millisecond thing

01:31:31.660 --> 01:31:32.630
where it's in your mouth,

01:31:32.630 --> 01:31:34.089
and it's almost like,

01:31:34.089 --> 01:31:36.650
what is your knee-jerk unconscious reaction?

01:31:36.650 --> 01:31:37.870
Like swallow or spit?

01:31:37.870 --> 01:31:39.779
Like that can go either way.

01:31:39.779 --> 01:31:42.304
Yeah, yeah.

01:31:42.304 --> 01:31:46.570
And every alcoholic justification was going through my head

01:31:46.570 --> 01:31:48.500
of like, oh, I already got a drop down my throat,

01:31:48.500 --> 01:31:51.672
it's already tainted or whatever.

01:31:51.672 --> 01:31:53.480
But I never did.

01:31:53.480 --> 01:31:54.850
I didn't take a drink.

01:31:54.850 --> 01:31:55.683
As soon as I got off stage,

01:31:55.683 --> 01:31:57.220
I pounded a couple of bottles of water.

01:31:57.220 --> 01:32:02.060
And that made me realize that the cravings that I've had,

01:32:02.060 --> 01:32:04.579
where I like really want to drink,

01:32:04.579 --> 01:32:06.960
that's not what's going on.

01:32:06.960 --> 01:32:10.260
I really don't want to drink, because I had,

01:32:10.260 --> 01:32:13.330
I had everything teed up for the perfect excuse

01:32:13.330 --> 01:32:15.850
where I had accidentally taken a drink.

01:32:15.850 --> 01:32:17.230
And then it would have been so easy.

01:32:17.230 --> 01:32:19.700
I'm on tour, like in a foreign country.

01:32:19.700 --> 01:32:24.700
I had every excuse lined up to justify a good relapse,

01:32:24.920 --> 01:32:26.530
and I didn't do it.

01:32:26.530 --> 01:32:30.520
And I realized it's because I really don't want to drink.

01:32:30.520 --> 01:32:35.520
And when I get those cravings about, I really want to drink,

01:32:36.419 --> 01:32:39.393
it's I miss being young.

01:32:40.230 --> 01:32:42.380
I miss being 22.

01:32:42.380 --> 01:32:45.220
I'm having a hard time dealing with aging.

01:32:45.220 --> 01:32:46.640
Being like a 40 year old man.

01:32:46.640 --> 01:32:47.473
It's fucking...

01:32:47.473 --> 01:32:48.390
It's really bombing.

01:32:48.390 --> 01:32:51.070
It's a romantic attachment or relationship

01:32:51.070 --> 01:32:53.430
with a bygone era

01:32:53.430 --> 01:32:55.980
in which you were carousing around Brooklyn and New York,

01:32:55.980 --> 01:32:57.240
like doing whatever.

01:32:57.240 --> 01:32:58.646
Yeah, yeah.

01:32:58.646 --> 01:33:01.910
It feels like a time machine in a bottle

01:33:01.910 --> 01:33:04.370
where I can just have a drink and go back there,

01:33:04.370 --> 01:33:06.210
to being a kid, to hanging out with my friends,

01:33:06.210 --> 01:33:07.950
to falling down in the river,

01:33:07.950 --> 01:33:10.383
or whatever bullshit we used to do.

01:33:13.070 --> 01:33:15.110
There's an adage in sobriety that,

01:33:15.110 --> 01:33:17.730
if you're truly sober, you can go anywhere.

01:33:17.730 --> 01:33:21.934
Like, you shouldn't have to avoid this place or that place

01:33:21.934 --> 01:33:24.593
because it might trigger you in a certain way.

01:33:25.596 --> 01:33:28.780
But that there has to be kind of a purpose

01:33:28.780 --> 01:33:33.470
for wandering into a location that you might imperil

01:33:33.470 --> 01:33:38.210
the solidity of your sober program.

01:33:38.210 --> 01:33:41.250
And what always mystifies me is that you do this alone.

01:33:41.250 --> 01:33:42.480
Like, I can't do it alone.

01:33:42.480 --> 01:33:43.440
I need other people.

01:33:43.440 --> 01:33:46.290
I need the secret society

01:33:46.290 --> 01:33:48.900
and everything that comes with that to stay sober.

01:33:48.900 --> 01:33:51.360
And I know what it's like to step outside that,

01:33:51.360 --> 01:33:53.760
and I've done so at my peril and it hasn't worked out.

01:33:53.760 --> 01:33:55.580
So, I stay close,

01:33:55.580 --> 01:34:00.580
but you still are holding onto this sort of self-will run,

01:34:02.380 --> 01:34:05.050
self-styled approach to sobriety.

01:34:05.050 --> 01:34:06.480
And I have no judgment on that.

01:34:06.480 --> 01:34:07.960
You're eight years sober, man.

01:34:07.960 --> 01:34:09.650
Like, that's amazing.

01:34:09.650 --> 01:34:12.840
But it's curious to me how you're able to do that,

01:34:12.840 --> 01:34:17.180
especially when your lifestyle finds you

01:34:17.180 --> 01:34:20.840
in all kinds of locations that aren't conducive

01:34:20.840 --> 01:34:23.160
to the highest vibration.

01:34:23.160 --> 01:34:27.433
Rich, know that you're with me everywhere I go.

01:34:28.447 --> 01:34:31.690
I mean, if you're not there, you're still there.

01:34:31.690 --> 01:34:34.790
You and a couple of other friends are like definitely people

01:34:34.790 --> 01:34:37.970
who I carry on my shoulder, in my head,

01:34:37.970 --> 01:34:40.050
with me wherever I go.

01:34:40.050 --> 01:34:41.470
'Cause I have moments of weakness

01:34:41.470 --> 01:34:45.350
and I definitely have moments of nihilism.

01:34:45.350 --> 01:34:48.350
Words like my nihilism it's in regression,

01:34:48.350 --> 01:34:50.580
but it can easily come back, you know?

01:34:50.580 --> 01:34:53.933
And I just think that,

01:34:58.120 --> 01:35:00.470
that's not the life that you would want for me.

01:35:00.470 --> 01:35:01.530
You would be worried.

01:35:01.530 --> 01:35:04.590
You would be concerned that...

01:35:05.690 --> 01:35:07.270
You know, I mean, there's a lot of things.

01:35:07.270 --> 01:35:09.900
Listen, I know at this point

01:35:09.900 --> 01:35:13.880
that I couldn't alienate you as a friend if I tried.

01:35:13.880 --> 01:35:15.650
But I know too that if I started drinking

01:35:15.650 --> 01:35:19.348
that it would be really hard for us to be friends, you know?

01:35:19.348 --> 01:35:22.249
And I have other friends like that.

01:35:22.249 --> 01:35:25.450
So I don't go into these places alone.

01:35:25.450 --> 01:35:28.383
I bring you and a couple other people with me.

01:35:33.340 --> 01:35:34.173
All right, we did it.

01:35:34.173 --> 01:35:35.880
This concludes today's deep dive

01:35:35.880 --> 01:35:38.080
into addiction and recovery.

01:35:38.080 --> 01:35:40.060
Thank you for taking this journey with me.

01:35:40.060 --> 01:35:43.430
I hope you not only enjoyed it, but found it helpful.

01:35:43.430 --> 01:35:45.480
And once again, in closing,

01:35:45.480 --> 01:35:49.588
if you or someone you care for is currently struggling,

01:35:49.588 --> 01:35:52.730
don't wait, please reach out for help.

01:35:52.730 --> 01:35:55.540
Towards this end, we have compiled a series of resources,

01:35:55.540 --> 01:35:57.010
which you can find in the show notes

01:35:57.010 --> 01:36:00.080
on the episode page at richroll.com.

01:36:00.080 --> 01:36:02.080
If you found today's video valuable,

01:36:02.080 --> 01:36:04.140
please subscribe to my channel,

01:36:04.140 --> 01:36:05.750
and I'll see you in a few days

01:36:05.750 --> 01:36:07.872
with another great episode.

01:36:07.872 --> 01:36:09.136
Peace.

01:36:09.136 --> 01:36:11.719
(upbeat music)

